[{"id": "36a0d3e3-67c8-438f-91a6-74cf1c233757", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:36): Interesting. So just to maybe paint a clear picture, I'm curious, you're talking about this pendulum swinging from extreme loyalty based business to an extreme acquisition based business. Can you give us two examples and maybe describe those polarities?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (16:52): Okay, so this is going to sound quite hilarious to you and probably to your listeners. They might say, oh man, what a weird world that I live in. But Hans Moser developed what is again in lore, and I'm going to send you a book to read on this call. I think you'll find it quite interesting. They actually say in Laura that Hans created one of the first loyalty programs on which programs free flyer programs were built on. There's a segment of a book that talks about the president of United Airlines was actually in one of our lodges and was learning about our Million foot program and was like, man, we need to do something like that. Anyways, again, lore, I don't know whether it's fact, but anyways, Hans realized that the nature of our guests are we are vert hungry in the wintertime, we want to go ski as much as we can because we just want to chew up powder. So he created this loyalty program where when you reach a million feet,\n\nCole Heilborn (18:10): This is elevation feet.", "token_estimate": 322, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "16:36", "end_timestamp": "18:10", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "06323e7c-6843-4ad6-b91c-b572156868dc", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (18:13): This is a million feet of skiing. So actually of going, so when you've skied a million vertical, you become part of a club. And traditionally back in the day, a million footer, well, it started out actually a million footer, got a green blazer similar to the masters. But we realized that that probably wasn't really aligned to what we do in terms of the winter is in skiing. So we morphed it and a million footer used to get this special one piece ski suit. And over the years, one pieces have come in and out of favor, and there was a point in time where we actually said, so it became a jacket and pants, and we decided this might've been in 2013 that these guests only got a jacket. So to put this into perspective, in order to ski a million feet with CMH, it takes roughly eight to 10 trips. (19:19): That is when we're looking at the price points of our trips, that's upwards of $200,000. And this is just for your first million. We have two three. Our guest with the highest racked vertical at CMH is 26 million. So these are arguably our most dedicated guests. They spend an exorbitant amount of money. We know all of us in marketing know the power of word of mouth. They often fill lodges with their friends, family, et cetera. And we decided that while we're really appreciative, we're only going to give you a jacket, we're going to take pants away because we need to redeploy the cost, the money we spend on pants to somewhere else in the business. And it was basically like we had murdered their children. We took their first child away from them. And when I came in, I said, for the money, we're saving on providing these extremely loyal guests a pair of pants. (20:37): The damage we're doing is so massive. And for us internally, again, we spent so much time, we spent so much time nurturing our guests, working with our guests. We know when a guest calls our office in Banff, we know their dog's name, we know what universities their kids are going to. We know a lot about these people. And we made a financial decision that broke all of our hearts, and it was a very simple sort of a minor actual financial impact. And so we actually brought pants back and it was kind of hilarious. The way we did it is basically the year after I, so for the winter after I started 20 15, 16 season, for any guest that had traveled with us the previous year, any million photo that had traveled with us the previous year and didn't get their pants but had booked, we're going to travel with us the following year, we just left a pair of pants on their bed and it said, we think you forgot something. We put a little label on it, and it was a bit tongue in cheek, but it also said, Hey, you know what? We're really sorry. We recognize that you are a very, very important part of our business and we messed up. So I think that's an example of where we had that pendulum swung so far over to acquisition. We were so focused on new people that we kind of lost our way a little bit.", "token_estimate": 751, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "18:13", "end_timestamp": "18:13", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "3cd1acd7-0c9a-47e2-a5b7-9ee2d9b772c9", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:13): Yeah, interesting. So the money that you were saving on pants, was that being allocated towards acquisition new clients or who knows,\n\nJulia LoVecchio (22:20): Maybe I didn't really worry about it. I was like, we're bringing pants back because the representation, again, because I have the wonderful fortune of being able to sell arguably a high luxury, high touch product and experience in the world, the actual money we are saving, if you translated it to the cost of a trip, I mean, I was like, this is, we just need to sell X number of private trips and we've made that up. And to be clear, the same guests that are buying our private trips are the ones that we just took pants away from, and I'm not really sure that that's the best use of our dollars. But then conversely, on the acquisition side, one of the elements that we were missing was a content marketing mindset, creating content for the wide range of guests that we're seeing not only even the wide range of guests that are booking with us right now, but content that will excite and create an emotional connection with our guests that are thinking about coming with us in 10 years. (23:46): And that is a piece that I think we where the pendulum when it was over on pure acquisition, absolutely. But when we're coming back into the center and trying to find that balance between loyalty and acquisition, we need to be firing with content on all cylinders. So yes, we're going to the content of a million foot suit, absolutely. We're going to keep that going because that what matters. The fact that we know our guests names, that we know their kids' names, all that, that element, that's all content that really matters to these guys. But over here, being super active on social was something that we really weren't partnering with. Some of the highly, I don't like using it highly influential, but also highly relevant athletes in our space. Someone like Travis Rice doing a project with him was while, yes, a good portion of our guests actually are skiers, Mount Snowboarders. He's someone that transcends the winter sports industry and creating a film completely with him was quite revolutionary for us, and I know we are seeing the benefits of it right now.", "token_estimate": 563, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "22:13", "end_timestamp": "22:20", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "d5d48827-221b-4535-9416-4f365e727e6d", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:11): Yeah, interesting. Can you speak to those results of that project? Was that content project one of the bigger projects that you'd taken on at the time in this effort to balance the pendulum?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (25:23): Yeah, I mean, I think it was a combination of, so Travis came for in my first spring. My first spring. So I started in March, in the middle of April. We put on a global sales meeting in our G lodge just south of Revels Stoke in a place called Trout Lake and put on a global sales meeting where I had to present my massive strategy that I was going to come up with. And I really was like, well, I don't even know where the bathroom is, so we'll see how we do. But just prior to that, we actually were able to host a GoPro athlete camp. This was when GoPro and I think they are still doing this, where GoPro would bring a sport specific or a seasonal specific group of people to a place to teach them how to use their equipment so that they can create content. And we were fortunate to host a winter athlete camp in 2015 in the spring, and was the first time that Travis had ever actually come to CMH. And he came up to me at the end of the week and he was like, wow, I had no idea. (26:46): I know if I want to film in bc, you guys are one of the companies that I need to be working with. And so that kind of started a conversation with us. Ultimately, we ended up creating a film that is going to be a couple week little web episodes, et cetera, turned into a feature length film, filmed entirely in Galena. Does Galena speak for itself? 100%. It does. It's got a very loyal fan base, but I will tell you, you can barely get a trip at Galena right now. It is probably sold out for the next 10 years. Is that as a result of that content piece? Maybe partly it is, but again, I just told this story actually this morning. I was on a bus with a guest going into one of our other lodges, and he pulled me aside and he said, that film project you did, that film just does not, I don't even understand it. (27:52): It was so dumb. And I looked at him and I said, listen man, I love you and thank you for this feedback, but it wasn't intended for you. You've been skiing with us for 30 years, you're in that film was for other people. That film is for other people to see what we can do here and that the world is changing and that these landscapes are important to everyone. And I said, I respect that. You didn't appreciate it. It really wasn't for you. So I think, again, it's creating content that's relevant for all people. And also it was a massive source of pride for our own internal team that one of the biggest names in snowboarding chose us to come and film. And again, from an alignment perspective, to have that pride internally to say, yeah, we created this together is pretty profound.", "token_estimate": 709, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "25:11", "end_timestamp": "25:23", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "c7502533-a60b-41c3-bfd1-67a376aa2720", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:50): So you mentioned that in your mind the concept of an internal and an external brand really is a poor way to think about it. And if there is truly a difference between your internal brand and your external brand, then there's probably some big problems going on because ideally they should be aligned, they should be the same, they shouldn't even be two separate things. I'm curious, when you stepped into the company and it was in this flux of trying to figure out how do we balance between acquisition and loyalty, were there differences between the internal brand and the external brand that the team has had to try to realign and make sure that they're pulling in the same direction?", "token_estimate": 178, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:50", "end_timestamp": "28:50", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "3a76f942-4713-42c8-aaff-19215b42013a", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (29:30): I don't know whether there were actually discreet differences. I think that we weren't telling our story. I think we were so focused on telling the story of the life-changing mountain experiences we created for our guests. We were forgetting how important the interaction was with our staff and actually telling that story as well. So it's almost like, it's funny to say, it's almost like we needed to get our mojo back around saying, yeah, we were first, we did create this industry. We have an enormous sense of pride in the people that work for us, that we are among the best. And that while yes, we host some of the most successful and accomplished, and our guests are incredibly talented people, our guests also can go anywhere and do anything around the globe. And they choose, not only do they choose, they dream about spending that week or two or whatever it is, their whole year is almost centered around coming and playing in the snow with us. (30:56): And that the snow, while the snow is extremely important, it's the interactions that they have sitting around the table at dinner with lifelong mountain guides, with hospitality professionals that understand how to engage in conversation that we can sit at the table. A king or a queen of a small country sits at the table at the same person at dinner that has changed their bed in the morning. There is not actually that much of a separation between all of us. And so we looked and said, we need to start celebrating ourselves just as much as we're celebrating our guests. We put together a film called Heartbeats that celebrated our lead pilot, one of our lead guides at Lead pilot at Lina, one of our lead guides at the Bobby Burns and one of our longtime lodge managers at Lina. And it was, well, yes, it was focused on, again, on acquisition and getting to know us. It also was a source of pride for our teams to say, yeah, look at the depth that we have, look at the amount of time we've spent understanding this industry and building this industry and that well, yeah, it's a pinnacle experience as a paying customer. It's also a pinnacle experience of a staff member. So I think it's not that. It's actually that the stories that exist within your business are just as important to tell as what the expectation could be of consuming your product.", "token_estimate": 586, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "29:30", "end_timestamp": "29:30", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "76c6e05c-7dc2-43be-bee2-b506dd294d20", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:43): Why is that important? Why was that important for CMH to understand and then start to act on? Was there a problem that that was creating or maybe a problem isn't the right word?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (32:55): I think it was a gap. I don't think it was a problem. I think it was a gap without people. We really are not a product. The snow is amazing when it's amazing, but when the snow is not amazing, and again, we operate in the wilderness. We are the end of the day, we we're farmers, we hope, hope that we don't have a crazy fire season. And when we go hiking that we're not surrounded in smoke. We hope that it doesn't rain for 30 days in the summertime, but it is what it is. And so when the environment is challenging, the only thing we have is our people. And the incredible amount, the depth and the relationships that we build, that's actually why people come back. Yeah, skiing, hiking, those activities, they're great. But when I watch a Wall Street investment banker get out of a helicopter and actually start weeping because they've been so moved, yes, they are moved by the mountains, but they're really moved by the experience that we've created. And we really needed to celebrate that. We really needed to celebrate that these people are part of the product, the people behind the product are the product.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:31): So obviously as a service-based business, as you said, the helicopters will be there, the snow will hopefully be there, but really what's always present is the people and embracing the people's stories and telling the people's stories of your business is so important. I'm curious from a product based business where maybe the individuals aren't as customer facing, do the same lessons apply?", "token_estimate": 440, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "32:43", "end_timestamp": "34:31", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "911e5b6f-0cee-4b0c-b9cb-69a3a8fb6f5d", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (34:57): I think they do, especially in a modern world where people are voting with their pocketbooks and they want to know that there's substance behind a product, that an employer, an organization treats their people well, that they invest in them, that they believe in them, that they care. I think more and more the marketplace is demanding that there is alignment between the two. And I think there's some really great marketing that's happened that causes us to believe that things are aligned. But sometimes when you lift up the lid, it's not the case. And again, I do not know this from firsthand experience, but there was a while where we were all hearing about the 80 20 time rule at Google, and it was like, yeah, you're allowed to pitch an idea and you're allowed to spend 20% of your time focused on that. But actually, you talk to people that work at gold, they're like, yeah, no, it just means you work a hundred percent of the time and then you work 140% on your other piece. And really, people are working really hard. And again, I don't know whether that's true or not, but it's almost like we don't want to be an organization that looks amazing from the front, but then when you walk around the back, it's just absolute chaos. And we're not perfect. I'm not saying we've achieved that, because I think in hospitality, oftentimes our intention is to create this incredible experience. And yeah, you're tired at the end of the day and you just put on a smiley face, but I think we're moving in the right direction for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:11): What's one of the biggest lessons that, I mean, you've been in marketing for a while, you've obviously learned quite a few lessons, but I'm curious if there's any particular lessons that you've learned from the last seven years at CMH?", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:57", "end_timestamp": "37:11", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "9dd25f8a-f890-4ca9-bf58-29b7bd6ca1e1", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (37:23): I think don't take yourself too seriously is a really important one. Laughter is extremely important. No matter what you do, it's probably the thing that keeps me going the most. So that's one. I think the second is get to know your customers. Ask. Don't be afraid to ask questions, and don't be afraid to hear answers that you weren't necessarily expecting, because every time you talk to someone, they give you just a little piece of insight into whether or not you are meeting their expectations. And I would say, again, ask your staff. Ask your staff those same questions, because the more we know each other and the more we understand each other, the better we're going to be ultimately because we need each other. And they are the only true. Your staff and your guests are the only true keepers of that information. We can do as much market research, external research. We can read every Harvard Business Review article out there, but if we're not actually asking the people that work for us and come and spend time with us, we're kind of doing things in the vacuum. And maybe that sounds like, well, it's marketing 1 0 1, but I think we need to remind ourselves about that.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:55): Yeah, maybe it's so simple that we forget about it.", "token_estimate": 321, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "37:23", "end_timestamp": "38:55", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "02be4957-b704-495b-b624-80565edccefe", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (38:59): And I think sometimes we think we're the experts, oh, we got this. I've been selling this stuff for seven years. I got it. I know exactly what we need to do. And it's like, well, maybe you don't, and it's okay to be wrong. I mean, one thing we noticed is we had this very, very, very formal language. I think there was a piece of us that said, oh, well, we're in the luxury market. We need to use luxury terms. And ultimately we're talking to skiers, snowboarders, and hikers. It's okay to be a little bit more casual, but we're not going all the way over to Brobra. (39:53): We need to stay somewhere in the middle where we pay respect to the people that came before us. But we also know that the future, we need to find that we need to find that fine balance. So I think we took our language almost so casual that we are like, Ooh, we need to bring it back. We're about to launch a new website. Hopefully it's been a long process. Those websites are hard to build, but I mean a massive learning, when we looked at our existing site and listeners, depending on when you hear this, our new site may be launched, but we spend a lot of time on our site talking about ourselves, our areas, our skiing, our terrain, and we've changed that narrative to you, your experience. And I would say the same for our staff and our guests. Your experience as a staff member of CMH is this and ourselves or putting our audiences as the central character is a big one. And again, marketing 1 0 1, but sometimes we forget and doing an audit of it is like, Ooh.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:16): Yeah. Oh, that's good. I mean, that is classic. It's like position your guest as the hero and we're the guide. And in your case, you're a literal guide. Yeah,", "token_estimate": 438, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:59", "end_timestamp": "41:16", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "105a69db-3cc8-4302-bb91-eda8d6cc60e7", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (41:25): Yeah, actually. Totally. Totally. I mean, it is the guiding, I think I would say that's a place where we still have a lot of work to do is we guide you through life-changing mountain experiences, and we're really, really good at doing that for our guests. Arguably, we're some of the best in the business. You look at our NPS core, I would say that that would, but are we guiding our staff through a life-changing experience at working for CMH? And what are the lessons we can take from the guiding industry and apply to us as leaders in a business? And that's way easier said than done. And I'd say that's probably the next 50 years of our existence is going to be about that.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:32): Why is that so important?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (42:34): Because without your people, you're nothing literally from the person at the airport that meets a guest to the team member that's writing copy for the new website to the chef that's in a lodge, preparing a meal to the guide that's outside hiking around in the wilderness, right up to the president of the company. Without them, we really do have no product and marketing, I mean, literally, we have nothing to market without our humans. So it's actually quite liberating. It gives you, I would say, as a storyteller, it gives me so many more stories to tell when we add our staff into and our personalities into our content. It's incredible because skiing is skiing, hiking is hiking. I mean, people can have incredible experiences a lot of places, but that content without those humans, I mean, it's just like, and the things that you find out about people when you ask questions, you're like, you're doing that. It's incredible.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:10): So how do you take all this forwards into the future", "token_estimate": 447, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "41:25", "end_timestamp": "44:10", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "9711e6cc-785c-444e-9927-592f912c1531", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (44:13): Many hours? I think those of us that work and live in the hospitality space right now are seeing that the great resignation, I think you guys maybe are experiencing it a little worse in the United States than we are in Canada. I don't know why, but that's not for me to decide. I really think even what we're seeing in content for conferences and leadership development and marketing strategy is the pendulum has swung over to the side of, it's not only just happy employees, but it is fulfilled and inspired and less chaotic. Work environments breed loyalty and loyalty is not just, oh, you're going to work at the same place for 30 years. It's for the time that you are there and you know how they talk about your brand is what people say about you, and you left the room. I mean, your internal brand is what your employees say when they're sitting at the bar with their buddies. (45:33): So I really think for us, it is continuing to celebrate the people that host our guests, and it's continuing to celebrate that intersection between the two of the friendships and the camaraderie that get formed. That is what is the transformational point of CMH. And yes, I don't sell nuts and bolts at a hardware store. I sell heli skiing and heli hiking. So I have a really unbelievable canvas from which to work from, but great. So let's keep going and let's be an example for a hardware store to celebrate the guy that's selling the hardware, because I think that's where the magic happens.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:26): Yeah, no, awesome. This is great.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (46:29): I don't know whether, it's so funny, I'm like, I don't even know whether I'm answering these questions, but anyways. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 430, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "44:13", "end_timestamp": "46:29", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "2d13844b-ca41-456b-a8d7-563c77e1409d", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (46:36): Well, what I'm hearing is that there's so much history, there's so much legacy, and there's so much so room for improvement in the future. And I think what we're witnessing is you're right in the middle of these conversations, right in the middle of some of these realizations. And so even just being able to listen to you, I mean, I think you understand all this, but I think we're kind of hearing the processing behind the scenes and trying to understand what is happening in real time. So I appreciate the candidness because I think those aren't often conversations or thoughts that we can just jump into mid flow of consciousness and listen to. So I think it's encouraging, it's exciting to hear where the company has been and where it's going and the things that you're thinking about.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (47:25): Yeah, it's a process. And we are not in the dark related to staying relevant, understanding the world that we live in now, that the world is not what it was even two years ago, let alone 50 years ago. Things have changed, guest expectations have changed, staff expectations have changed. And if we don't ask our guests and our staff, if we don't carry those conversations on and be open to hearing their feedback, we won't be around for another 50 years and are we in a position? And I would challenge every chief marketing officer at every business on the planet right now to really push themselves on understanding what their role is in telling that story. And if they are only focused on telling the story of the widget, then they're kind of missing half of the probably 80% of really the story.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:46): Yeah, man,\n\nJulia LoVecchio (48:49): It's\n\nCole Heilborn (48:49): Heavy. It is heavy. It's good stuff.", "token_estimate": 442, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "46:36", "end_timestamp": "48:49", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "7109d652-6cb7-4e3a-8209-49c23e52a94f", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (48:52): Yeah. I know that I am extremely fortunate. I know that I get to play in a space that is, that's amazing. But how amazing is it that we actually understand that we do sell joy? I think that's incredible. And I think we all are craving joy right now. If I hear the world word pent up demand related to tourism, one more time, my head's going to explode, but I also, I get it. And how excited are we as a team? I remember when in November when we all got together for the first time in two years, our whole team came to Banff. All of our area managers, lodge managers, et cetera, came to math and we were finally together, and it was like, oh yeah, I love working with these people. So I think we have a responsibility to keep providing space for people to gather, keep providing places for joy, because the world is extremely heavy right now. So yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (50:12): And heli skiing and summer adventures, they aren't that difficult. You made me realize that anyone can go helis skiing. You don't have to be able to ski a double black.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (50:24): No.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:25): So really, everyone should be out.", "token_estimate": 290, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "48:52", "end_timestamp": "50:25", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "def08384-4959-4908-a238-3a06c4c5877e", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (50:29): It's a pretty simple formula. If you've got the time, you definitely need to have some resources, financial resources, if you have a really deep passion for skiing or snowboarding, we would say that you need a certain level of fitness. In fact, fitness is probably more important than skill, technical skill. But ultimately, if you've got a really open heart and mind to coming and spending time in the back country and you're willing to listen to where the guide tells you to go, absolutely. You can come heli skiing. We've got all sorts of tools for decision making on our website for people around that. But yeah, you don't need to be Travis Rice. That's definitely for sure. Nor do you need to be an Olympic caliber skier to come he skiing. And then in the summertime. I think probably the most rewarding thing that I've seen personally is a guest that loves the outdoors that might not be able to do what they used to be able to do, and that helicopter can drop them off on a glacier beside a glacier at a high alpine lake, and they can just sit there if that is what they really want to do. (52:01): The world is a very crowded place right now, and I think what we are able to do is get people into those really uncrowded spaces so they can focus on what matters to them.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:13): Yeah. Well, Julia, I want to thank you for your time today. If folks want to follow along with you personally, if they want to follow along with CMH, where can they go to connect\n\nJulia LoVecchio (52:25): For me? I would say my Instagram, and I apologize for the swearing at Jules, J-O-O-L-E-S, and of course you can always follow us at cm h he or cm cmh he.com, cmh summer.com, and we'd love to see you out. Come on up to Canada. Love to have all y'all.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:47): Awesome. Well, Julia, appreciate the conversation. I appreciate the candid thoughts. I've really enjoyed this. You've given me plenty of things to consider for my day and my week, so I look forward to staying in touch and seeing what the future looks like, how you guys continue to blaze a new path forwards or a semi old, but new and exciting path forward.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "50:29", "end_timestamp": "52:47", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "40f9e666-42bf-496b-968b-6dbb622590ab", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (53:10): Awesome. Thank you so much, Cole. I really want to say thank you for the opportunity. I think your podcast is really important, and for anyone that is interested in talking about this stuff, I'm pretty obsessed. Yeah, let's connect.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:27): Sounds good. Well, have a great rest of your day and we'll chat soon.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (53:31): Thank you very much.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:32): See you.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (53:33): Have a good one.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:36): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 177, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "53:10", "end_timestamp": "53:36", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "15e324df-2f8c-4ec3-9854-b058d3a5d457", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:00:03): At the end of the day when all the packaging is gone, logos are scraped off and you've forgotten even where you bought. Whatever it is that you have. There is nothing left of your brand for a customer, consumer user except for that artifact that they have products that are designed to last for more than disposability, the artifact is the brand. At some point, that product experience is all that you'll remember.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Josh Bessler. He's a design and strategy consultant over at Delicious. Josh, how are you?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:01:16): I'm great. How are you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:17): Doing awesome. I'm excited to be back in the podcast seat. It's been a little bit, took a little break, but here we are. We did it. We made it\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:01:25): Nice. Yeah, surviving this thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:31): Well, I'm super excited for our conversation today. We connected, gosh, it must've been a while ago, A couple months ago. We initially hit it off, and I'm looking forward to coming back to that conversation and actually recording it this time. So I think folks are going to be in for a fun conversation today. Josh, I'd love if you could first start by introducing yourself. Where are you calling in from and what do you do?", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:03", "end_timestamp": "00:01:31", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "d048d73e-767e-4756-bfae-d0eac9830fb1", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:01:55): Well, my name as you said is Josh Bessler, a mouthful of syllables there. I live in Seattle, Washington. I'm based in Pioneer Square. That's where I both live and work. I've been in Seattle for about 20 years. I went to school in Western Washington University, not too far from where you are in Bellingham. I'm originally from Alaska, born and raised, moved to Seattle in the mid nineties, came down here and stumbled myself into the industrial design program at Western, kind of through circumstance. Seattle was the straightest path to get out of Bellingham when I graduated. Came here and did some work in a couple of different places. I worked for a consultancy. It was my first gig. Ended up working at a cell phone company for a while and then worked at Cascade Designs. I did about a five year stint in house at Cascade Designs as an industrial designer, and I was given a very graceful exit in 2008 at the economic downturn, which was an easy path for me to start doing my own thing. I was literally having the conversation in the layoff discussion. (00:03:13): It was like a headlight lit up at that point. So I ended that conversation by proposing that they would probably still need some industrial design help and I would be happy to work with them. And not too many weeks after that, cascade became one of my first clients and has been for the last 15 years or 14 years. So that's the very quick and narrow version of how I got to this point with not too many steps in between. So I've been doing consulting on my own for the last 14, coming up on 15 years this year. So it's been quite a while in my gray strikes.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:03:51): Where in Seattle?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:03:52): I'm sorry?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:03:53): Where in Alaska did you grow up?", "token_estimate": 450, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:55", "end_timestamp": "00:03:53", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "9ec6cd42-5432-4ffd-a7ac-1c4b1f4f18b8", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:03:56): Predominantly Anchorage. I split time between Anchorage and a small town called Homer, which is at the end of the road as far as you can drive south effectively. So I spent one part of my life in the suburbs and one part of my life in the woods. No running water, outdoor toilet, the whole nine yards. So I have a fairly eclectic upbringing that's probably given me a unique level of comfort being in either place. I'm as happy as I am in the middle of Manhattan or on the top of a mountain somewhere. They both feel very at home to me. I hardly say Anchorage and Manhattan have anything in common.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:04:39): There's something, the history about the whole inside passage in southeast Alaska all the way down to Seattle is super rich, and especially I guess you being based in the Pioneer Square area growing up here in Bellingham, I'd always grow up with the ferry leaving twice a week to head up to Juneau and Ketchikan, and I don't know, it's an amazing little section I feel like of the country that doesn't get a lot of attention,\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:05:06): Very much so the weirdest experience for me in that transition was moving to Bellingham and going to see the ferry arriving at the terminal from the place that I was from. It's just a very strange sort of outof body experience to have.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:05:21): Yeah, yeah. There's only a few ways to get up there, and when there's a ferry that goes twice a week, there's something very romantic about it.", "token_estimate": 379, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:56", "end_timestamp": "00:05:21", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "3e21c2e6-01df-4ba4-be77-da7e00ae8c57", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:05:32): Yeah. Ironically, I've never done the ferry. I've driven twice in the middle of the winter in cars that were not well suited to do that drive. And it puts a point of perspective on just how far away Alaska really is. It's a three hour plane ride because it's a straight line. The trip from Seattle to Anchorage is roughly the same distance as Seattle to the tip of Florida. So you get a sense of really how much landmass you're crossing. And when you say you're from Alaska, it's like saying you're from the Midwest, like, well, which part has this massive expansive land? So there just aren't that many places in Alaska to be from. So that's an interesting thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:06:10): So I'm curious, how did you come up with the name for your business? Delicious.", "token_estimate": 200, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:05:32", "end_timestamp": "00:06:10", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "d26220e8-b282-49e0-9062-819199c1447f", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:06:13): Well, I had some early variations, first of which was just my name and nobody can spell that or pronounce it. I went with a couple of other things that had been legacy ideas I had played around with for years, and ultimately it popped into my head in the middle of a conversation with some colleagues, and I don't quite know what stuck with me, but it felt like a bit of a, it disrupted my thinking on what design was at the time, but it also, I was trying to distinguish myself. I needed a name that I could put on a software license that wasn't my name because it was too hard to spell or pronounce it and put in front of people that didn't also just sound like me. I don't want me to be the center of the studio. It is me, but it's far more interesting for it not to be me. (00:07:07): It gives me room to expand. Somebody else can show up and be delicious at some point, and that might be beneficial. But the other thing was most of the other agency names that I had seen out there had these very masculine sounding connotations at the time. There was Stratos, there was Synapse, there was, oh gosh, I don't know, strategics, and it went on and on and they just were like, I don't know. There's a little bit of an angularity and edge to it. And one of the things I really wanted to include in that thinking or that presentation of my brand so to speak, is something a little bit softer, something a little more approachable. I also realized that the business world, I'm going to sound like a grandfather here, but the business world was at the time and has now really changed into being a lot more inclusive of women who are in very powerful roles. (00:08:04): And I am working more with women in those roles. But I wanted something that felt a little more accessible to women also. And wasn't just this, I'm the man doing the man job with the man design. That isn't what I'm interested in. So it was an attempt to just build a bridge to something a little more sensory also, which speaks more to the way I think about design at the end. It's just a name. I think they all are, and it isn't like I have a belief that design should be delicious. I mean, that doesn't even make any sense. My banker looks at me and says, so you're eating food? And I say, we've had this conversation six times, but the idea of design being delicious and what that could mean too, it's not so linear. It really talks about it accesses it from a different point for me. And if your design is delicious, well, what might that be like for you? That would to me describe an experience of some sort that's kind of juicy and rich? So I want my customers and my clients to have juicy and rich experiences. So here we're, and I'll never say the words juicy and rich next to my business name ever again.", "token_estimate": 702, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:06:13", "end_timestamp": "00:06:13", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "3e3bd63e-27f3-4f23-8e3f-d33366db92e3", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:09:19): Interesting. So I'm curious if you've been doing this for quite a while now, you said 15 years on your own, what are some of the products that, I guess, what does a typical process look like? So you get brought in to help design a product for a company, or what does that look like?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:09:37): It really depends. Oftentimes, well, let's say early in my consulting career, I got brought in to design a thing. Somebody needed a product solution, and there I was. And by virtue of being there and being the kind of designer that I am, and I'll back us into this tangent momentarily, but I would be compelled to ask questions like, well, namely, why are we doing this? What is your intent in this product? Or what's the purpose of this thing? But eventually ask enough questions that I would push that conversation into more of a strategic thinking conversation for lack of a better term. And so I built my strategy consulting by asking a lot of irritating questions to clients when I was asked to just do a simple product. And by asking for more information, it sometimes peeled the lid back on the fact that they hadn't really thought it through that well. And we really needed to do a further exploration to understand what it was, what problem we were really trying to solve, or for whom it was being solved or what market, et cetera. (00:10:44): These days, I often get the same requests. I also get requests to come in and help with some sort of strategic thinking. And we can argue till we're blue in the face over whether this is strategy or tactics or where that falls in the hierarchy, but let's just say strategy for a loose term. I get asked to help develop product line plans, look at larger inclusive group framing exercises to bring in a body of people who come from all different disciplines like sales, marketing, engineering, design, et cetera, to come to consensus on what we should be doing. And I think as my gray hair increases, I have, this isn't visual, but I don't look particularly conventional. I guess I have sort of unusual haircut and big earrings, et cetera. So the gray hair sort of gives me some cred that I might occasionally know what I'm talking about. (00:11:45): And I find that one of the things that is important me and also valuable to my clients is I build a pretty good degree of trust that what I say I believe in, I'm not just going to sell somebody the answer they're looking for, interested in just telling them what they want to hear that doesn't benefit me or them. And so I tell them what I think is most important for them to hear and what I think is most true to the right path. And by building that over time, I get asked to come back and do more and more. I get more and more involved in the overall process. So not just product but strategy. I've led sales meetings, which is very weird. I don't know the first thing about sales, but I know how to speak to the audience and bring them through the design thinking and to advocate for how design can be used in the larger business context.", "token_estimate": 771, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:09:19", "end_timestamp": "00:09:37", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "9ca63a38-d186-4ec7-8370-483b79b8afae", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:12:44): Gotcha. And so folks just have a little bit of a grasp. What are some of the products that you have worked on over the last few years?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:12:52): Well, it's all over the place. I don't have a particular focus. I think there was a time when I was pretty heavily in outdoor. I think that's shifted quite a bit, but I've designed most of the hard goods for Cascade Designs for the last 18 years. So the reactor stove is a highlight for me for one of the more iconic products that I think has gotten really good reception in the market. But most of the stoves that as far as gone, I've had a hand in the Guardian water filter most recently came up as a design that I did that has won some awards. Apparently. (00:13:35): I've more recently worked with Jonathan Wagner, who was on one of your earlier podcasts, and we revamped the entire SOG knife line. So most of the knives that have come out in the last couple of years have my hand on 'em. Gosh, what else? That probably covers some pillar products. I think that would be interesting to your listeners. I want to be especially clear here, and I'll reinforce this constantly, that I had a hand in these things, but I have no sense of ownership of them. It's a team of people always, right? I'll always say that, but it's really true that none of these things that I'm most proud of could have come together without a bunch of people could. The name, the list of names is so long, it goes on forever. But there are some really key engineers and brand people and marketing people and salespeople and so on have made those tremendously successful and influenced the outcomes as much as I did. But I'm very proud to say that I've worked on those things and contributed to that and guided some of that process as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:14:43): Yeah. What makes a good product for you?\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:14:49): Oh, man, that's a tough question. I think what makes a good product is a good process. I think what makes a good product is", "token_estimate": 505, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:44", "end_timestamp": "00:14:49", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "00616e19-95c7-48d7-ba13-013708ff323e", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:15:02): A good team of people that can bring that through a process. Sometimes the process isn't good, but if the team of people is good, it makes up for it. Sometimes the team of people isn't the team you hope for. Sometimes process can help with that, but I find that there is no thing that makes a product good. What makes a product good is whether\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:15:27): Or not it effectively delivers on the brand promise that is being made, period.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:15:39): If you're making toilet paper, which is the most mundane, uninteresting, and banal thing that I can think of in this current moment, and your toilet paper is not pleasant to use for the variety of reasons that we might imagine, it's not pleasant to use. You have failed in making toilet paper. So if your brand is soft and lovely toilet paper and you didn't make that, your product is a failure, if your brand is something else and you delivered the wrong direction, it's the same thing. So I think across the board, I know that's a very generic answer, but I can't think of a more broad way to communicate that Your product has to deliver the promise that your brand is making, period. And if it fails on that, it's a failure of a product period.", "token_estimate": 313, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:15:02", "end_timestamp": "00:15:39", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "30468a76-8620-4d56-8589-9ff7bb4412b6", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:16:26): On this show, something that we've always there, there's tons of different topics that we've discussed in the field of marketing, but one thing that we've never really discussed is good marketing is built on you have to have a good product in order to actually market it. And this is why I'm really excited to kind of dive into this conversation with you because that foundational element really sets the whole marketing team up for them to be able to go do their job. So when you're talking about a good product, it reflects the brand, it reflects the brand's values. I guess kind of walk me through, you're brought into a meeting and you're talking to a new client, and I guess I'm curious, what are some scenarios or situations where you get a sense that maybe this new product that's trying to be brought to life isn't in line with brand? And how do you feel that out? What are some things that you notice? How do you tell when a product is in line?", "token_estimate": 245, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:26", "end_timestamp": "00:16:26", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "28f923c5-ba7d-49e6-9781-492f8a585aee", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:17:21): Can I back us up? One question and okay. You said that you don't often talk about product in a marketing podcast as I'm not terribly surprised, but I'm a little bit chagrined to say that. I'll just back up and say this first thing. At the end of the day when all the packaging is gone, the logos are scraped off and you've forgotten even where you bought whatever it is that you have, there is nothing left of your brand for a customer, consumer user except for that artifact that they have. And I'm talking about physical products. Obviously digital stuff is sort of indelible, but in the physical world, products that are designed to last for more than the disposability, the artifact is the brand. At some point, that product experience is all that you'll remember. And the best success of brand is that your logo lasts long enough that the person can go back and find another one under the same brand, right? (00:18:30): So I mean, that's even where the brand came from originally, right? The insignia on livestock effectively was what brand meant. And so that was how you knew you had this farmer's cow versus that Farmer's Cow. That's the a al story. So this seems like I'm highly biased here. I'm a product designer, make products. I think about brand and marketing and all these things in terms of product, and I understand that there's all kinds of other parts that go into that, and those are equally important, but the communication, even the outward facing customer points should all be pointing back to what that product experience is going to be like. So I think to go back to your question of how do I know, how do I get a sense that a product isn't on brand, there are pieces in front of that. It would be unusual for me to encounter a product where it was just patently not in line with the brand. (00:19:34): I'm not sure that I've ever actually seen that. What I've seen is a product brief or request that didn't quite fall into line with what intuitively seemed like the right product line strategy or maybe didn't feel like the right fit for the brand. And I'm not sure that I can give you a discreet example of that, but I can give you sort of an abstract. If there's a car company that's suddenly is putting a brief in front of me for a scooter, this has never happened. But here's the hypothetical, I'm going to ask some hard questions, but where are you going with scooters and are you sure that your customers can understand that? Can BMW make a scooter? Well, probably it has wheels, but boy is the ultimate driving machine now distilled down to a, I mean, yes, the answer is probably, but it's going to lead to a lot of questions and conversation about how are you going to support this and how are you going to bring your customers into this new landscape, and can you really deliver on this promise that you're making with all the rest of your products? Or does this reinforce that or is this dilute that approach? (00:20:42): More of the textbook type case for me is that I'm brought into to consider a product line rather than just a product. And the product line may have holes in it where we're forcing a fit simply to fill in a gap that seems relevant because we have a matrix and the matrix says, well, there's a big hole here. Well, sometimes there's a big hole there because you shouldn't go there. I mean, holes generally imply that they don't need to be filled, you need to stay out of them. So whether you should be filling it or whether you should stay out of it is the kinds of questions that I'm trying to answer in those strategy conversations. And it's the typical look at the market, look at the customers, look at your legacy, look at what resources you have to support the kind of development you wanted. You look at what your brand says and all of those sorts of things. The constant question I'm asking in my head is, are you reinforcing or are you diluting? And not necessarily what those specific words, but that is the kind of thinking I'm doing around what products should be there and can you", "token_estimate": 1019, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:17:21", "end_timestamp": "00:17:21", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "ddd71c7d-126f-4e1b-9ef0-789e04e7754e", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Speaker 3 (00:21:46): Launch this new thing into that space?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:21:51): Can you attract the customers that you've already built in? Are you going to try to attract new customers? And ultimately, are you going to launch orphans or are you going to launch long arcs of products? Long arcs of products are much easier to support. I think orphans are kind of interesting. I mean, pulling rabbits out of hats is a fascinating exercise. And I can point to the knife industry is rich in companies pulling rabbits out of hats this year at the Knife Show. It's just whatever comes out of every pipeline and people who in awe because they want to see the novelty, but it goes all over the place. There's no theme, there's no consistency. And for a knife, it may not be that critical for an outdoor brand of some sort with more breadth to its product offerings that can be really challenging for customers. (00:22:42): The question is always, well, can I trust? Can I trust the next thing I'm going to buy? Can I trust the next thing I'm going to buy from this company that sold me a thing that was good and a good brand? And I'll pick obviously the easy low hanging for you. Let's say Apple is a brand that you can generally rely on to deliver really high quality hardware software. Well, sometimes, and when you buy a thing from Apple, there's an implicit trust that the hardware will be fantastic. It'll be the most sublime, most the Pinnacle hardware experience you'll probably have as a consumer. Well, if Apple fails to deliver on that, it is disastrous for their brand. I mean, they have a lot of momentum, they have a lot of customers, but it doesn't take long for the edges of that to start to erode. And pretty soon they're just selling commodity junk along with everybody else. So it's a great precious thing to maintain. And I treat that with great reverence. When I approach these conversations.", "token_estimate": 479, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:21:46", "end_timestamp": "00:21:51", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "b46708e3-d568-4ec7-b2f5-9f93a85ff119", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:23:42): That visual brings up an image in my head of there's a certain mass with each company and they have so much brand credibility and health and good products build on that poorly designed or poorly executed products, eroded that. And eventually over time, depending on the strength of that brand, they could eventually crumble if they release poor thing after poor thing. Maybe that's an extreme example.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:24:14): Well, case in point, I'll use Saga as an example because we watched it happen on the public forum thats had a history of making some products of maybe some questionable quality, at least to the perception of a bunch of users. And when we started revamping the brand, we saw a swell of comments that basically said, I haven't looked at a SOG knife in 22 years, and all of a sudden you have my attention. These better work as well as they say they're going to work, or I'll be really mad. But we got the attention of people who suddenly were tuned in and hadn't been for a long time because at some point they had been soured by some experience they had there and never wanted to go back. Well, that's telling them you've been burned once. It's very hard to come back. So it implies that one should be very cautious all the time, and at some point you do have to take some risks, but you take those calculated risks that you will still be able to deliver on the promises you've made and mitigate that risk however you choose. So I think SOG shows a pretty good example there. I can't say that I've ever designed a bad product because I would never say that, but the reality is that we haven't always hit success with every product I've ever designed, and those hurt tremendously, but we figure out ways to course correct as quickly as possible, either make amends, but make improvements, and then release something that brings people back into that fold.", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:23:42", "end_timestamp": "00:24:14", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "93c554e1-3637-4a3a-ae21-5405c0868b09", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:25:49): Yeah, interesting. You kind of set this up before we started recording, but there's the strategy side and then there's the design side. And I guess in my simplistic way of thinking about it, the strategy is should we bring this product to market? And then the design is what does this product actually look and feel and how does it operate to continue down this vein of the strategy side, sometimes companies will come out with, as you say, an orphan product or something out of left field and it totally works and you're like, wow, I can't think of an example right now, but you're like, this company just came out with this product. It's totally out there, but there's something about it that fits. How does that work?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:26:33): Man? Wow, that's a great question.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:26:40): And if you have an example, maybe that would help.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:26:43): Yeah. Well, it's really hard to find examples of this because there aren't many things that are left field these days. Most markets are really saturated, so most products are incremental, and the more saturated and the more mature a market gets, the more the next product is going to be iterative on the ones before it even within the marketplace. But man, that is the most fascinating question I've been asked in a long time, and I'm going to spend some time trying to answer this intelligently. So I think one way to answer this is that how would you describe this? You used a word on this. Would you say an orphan product? Is that\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27:29): Yeah, is what you would use to describe this?", "token_estimate": 406, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:25:49", "end_timestamp": "00:27:29", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "51343a4b-dbea-43f1-b141-d5b1870574d0", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:27:32): No, I was trying to use your language there. Let's say maybe it's even a unicorn product. It's something that shouldn't even exist. It's just there and it's staring you in the face and you don't know where it came from or how it got there, but it's the most amazing thing you've ever seen. Most of the time, those ideas are disastrous, and they're usually, there's crackpots and geniuses and they're right, they live right next door to each other and they look a lot the same. And the only difference is the geniuses tend to rise upward and the crackpots tend to just burrow in, but you're never really sure where you are. So I think coming up with a unicorn, it takes a tremendous vision. I have have been around this block enough times to have a pretty strong feeling that luck plays a significant role, but it proves itself out that if you just got blind, lucky, you can do that once, but you can't ever repeat it. You didn't know what you did. So I think it takes tremendous vision to have a\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:28:34): Successful unicorn. And that vision is usually fueled by a level of investment, insight, expertise, passion, things that most people don't possess frankly.", "token_estimate": 301, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:27:32", "end_timestamp": "00:28:34", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "b35da97e-1589-4c27-ab01-d672ffd2a198", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:28:56): And I think that I would describe those people as champions of that particular product in some way. They're the ones who will defend it, the ones who will fight for it, the ones who are out there really pushing it forward. And I'm sort of circling around this because I can't drive straight to the thing. I don't think they're exceptionally rare. And so because of that, they're figuring out how can you make an orphan product? I have no idea. Like I said, it takes somebody who has that vision and has that investment. And if you don't have that, you're just simply going to get lucky. So you're that person, you have that thing, you still have a lot of luck, still have to assemble the right team, get the right things together, and in a mature corporate context, the chances of coming up with that unicorn product are slimmer and slimmer. (00:29:58): The more mature and complex your organization gets, because you have more and more stakeholders that you have to bring in, you have more people to appease, you have more PowerPoint presentations to generate, you have more analysis and matrices and more eyeballs and more people's demands to satisfy. So I think this is why we sort of put a lot of emphasis on the startups and our current product culture because I think those are the places where you can break the rules. You're totally unfettered. You just have hopefully a pile of money and a lot of momentum and some visionary. And so we see places like Silicon Valley, the playground for this kind of stuff. And even then, not that many great things are coming out of there, relatively speaking. But let's say again, I'll pick some easy examples here. Steve Jobs was this kind of guy. (00:30:48): You can say what you will about all the rest of Steve Jobs and all the stories that had written about him, good and bad, but the guy had something clearly that no one else had. And when Steve Jobs left the building that went with him, I believe Steve Jobs probably built a roadmap that went way, way out into the future. And the roadmap that I would say now is seeing it start to run out. And what Apple can do well now is operations. Their CEO is an operations guy and he knows how to squeeze the last penny out of every single thing, and he does it exceptionally well. But the days of great vision and unicorn products, I think we're hoping Apple will get back to that. There's no back to get to the back is the guy that's gone now. So Steve Jobs comes out with, I don't know what picket an iPhone. (00:31:42): What was the thing before that that put Apple on the map, the iMac? I mean these things that he had a great sense that no, it should be this way. And everybody else in the room told him, I don't know, man, we don't think so. And he says, Nope, I don't care what you think. This is what it's going to be. And to have that kind of confidence and vision and madness and temerity to stake your entire company's fortune on that success or failure is extraordinary. It's truly extraordinary. So I think what also comes along with that, this is my pitch for myself here, is good advisors, people you trust that can sit close to you and kind of tell you whether you're out of your mind. It doesn't mean you have to listen to them, but if you don't listen to anybody, if fired your own editor so to speak, you're in dangerous territory. (00:32:33): You're on an island by yourself. And I don't know anybody that can successfully pull that off. And I think even Steve Jobs had people that he kept close that could give him good advice, give him good counsel, give him good feedback, and that's sort of where I come in. I'm not the visionary. I readily tell people this. I'm not here to give you the rabbit from the hat. I don't know what it is. You tell me what your strategy is. You tell me what your vision is. I'll tell you whether or not I think it can be useful to your brand. I can tell you whether or not it makes sense. I'm great for clarity of reason, but I'm not here to astonish you with the thing you've never thought of before, but I can take the idea that you have and then give you solutions you've never thought of before. (00:33:20): That's the difference, right? So I don't know if that totally answers your question, but I think that's the best answer. And then to bring that right to modern times, and again, the outdoor industry, I'm trying to come up with something that would be more relevant to this audience. The outdoor industry is really challenging, and I'll throw one idea out there in a second, but to pick another more obvious and mainstream one, Tesla, Elon Musk comes out with a cyber truck, puts it on stage, everybody's minds are blown because they've never seen a door wedge. Is it on four wheels before? And we all go, well, I don't know. Is that genius or madness? No one really knows Elon Musk publicly manifests that he might be a mad man. Then occasionally does genius things. And we don't know. Time will tell when that cyber truck actually ships, assuming it actually does. (00:34:10): We can talk about it then, but I don't know. There's a place where we just don't know Steve Jobs. I feel like he kind of knew whatever was coming next was going to be good. Elon Musk, you kind of know whatever's coming next is going to be a wild ride, and there's a big, big gap there. So the outdoor industry, I think one of the more interesting innovations that was a bit of a unicorn was jet oil. I have a certain client that probably hates this comment, but I don't think we can steer around it, that jet oil comes out with a pot that bayonets into a stove and looks surprisingly like a self coffee cup and the world goes nuts. And now everything, anything that looks like that is compared to and referred to as a jet boil, they came out of it practically nowhere to everybody's sensibilities. (00:35:04): It was a unicorn. How many others have we seen in the outdoor industry in the last decade? Not buried many. So they're vanishingly rare, and I don't know what came together for oil to do that, but I haven't seen Ju Oil do anything since then that has been more innovative than the original JU oil stove. It's iterations on the same thing, which tells me it might've just been luck. I don't really know. But if it was luck, well, they've done quite well with luck, but unicorns are unicorns for a reason. They're very hard to follow up with another unicorn. So I think they've done as well as they could possibly do with having one fantastic idea. And the natural next evolution was, well, you have to compete on the same playing field as everybody else at some point because you're still in something as mundane as stoves, not space shuttles. (00:36:05): So it follows that they would come up with stoves that are more like what the rest of the market is offering and more competitive. But jet oil still, MSR reactor sure looks a lot like a jet oil, not because we tried to make it look like that, but because a stove on a canister with a pot on top of it kind of has this vertical cylindrical character to it. It's pretty hard to avoid that. But how many people talk about it? They just call it a jet oil. All those things are a jet oil that's challenging.", "token_estimate": 1808, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:28:56", "end_timestamp": "00:28:56", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "815c0128-5e2e-45ee-9f3e-5e61f1255d19", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:36:38): In your example with Apple, and I'm thinking about brands and the foundation to a brand and for Apple Words, innovative, forward thinking, clean, all those words come to mind when I think of their brand. And we're talking about a unicorn product. And I guess my question though is in the instance like Apple, where that is their brand, where they're always at least they were always innovative and they're always coming out with something new, and it was kind of expected that every couple of years, every five years something new and mind blowing would come out. Is it really then a unicorn product if they've come out with the iMac or whatever, the Lisa, and then they come out with the Mac and then they come out with the iPod and then the iPhone and the iPad when their brand is built on innovation? Is that,", "token_estimate": 210, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:36:38", "end_timestamp": "00:36:38", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "89f78abf-144d-407a-9ca5-f568efadfa63", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:37:31): Yeah, I know this is a challenging thing. Is it even fair to say that a company like Apple is making unicorn products at all because it's sort of in their brand ethos. I mean, think different was their brand ethos for a long time, right? That's since changed and the products have, I think commensurate changed with it. And is the iPhone really a unicorn product? No. It was the evolution of a bunch of other stuff that was out there. What Apple does best is take the best technology that's going on right now, pull the pieces that are good, put it together into a package and put it in front of the consumer and say, look, you've never seen anything like this before. Even though you've seen lots of things like it, some of that's just the hustle, that's the marketing, but they're not wrong. You've never seen it presented this well. (00:38:21): You've never had an experience this good. And then they claim with as much authenticity as I think they can, that's novel and they might be right. But to your point, it might not be the best example of a true unicorn product where we just knocked us off our seats because we could never conceive of anything that unusual. But like I said, those things are, I can't even think of an example. They're so rare, and I'm sure there's one sitting right in front of us somewhere we go, oh yeah, it would be that. But those things I think also are oftentimes a technology, not a product. So there are technologies that there was a space that nothing existed and it suddenly exists because a bunch of people were all moving toward that spot at once. Then it gets turned into a product, it gets incorporated into a product. And oftentimes what happens is you see a bunch of those products all show up in the market in a relatively short order, reactor, jet boil, what have you, because everybody was all kind of thinking about similar things. There's first to market and then there's everybody else that unfortunately gets to be second and the first to market usually gets the rights to the minds share. They get the rights to people's understanding of what that is,", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:37:31", "end_timestamp": "00:37:31", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "9192d7ec-7843-4077-9b8a-3b8dd527cc2a", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:39:41): Assuming they do it well, sometimes the second\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:39:43): To they do well, right? Sometimes second to market. I never advised my clients to try to be first because I think there's greater value in being second and better than being first and worst, so to speak. Those aren't always mutually exclusive, but there's no failure in being second. Sometimes there can be greater successes there, but there are things you trade on.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:40:07): So when you're looking at a company's product lines and all of the different skews that they have, I'm curious for Josh personally, do you look at all the different lineups and say, man, I think they need to pair these down or they should keep expanding. And obviously this is on a very general level.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:40:29): The question is, would I advise a client to expand the product line or pare down\n\nCole Heilborn (00:40:36): Or even shrink the existing product lines? Let's say I have 10, is your natural reaction to say, let's focus on five really good ones and cut these others, or should we take these 10 and take it to 15?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:40:49): Yeah, that depends. That doesn't like the worst answer. That depends. Well, it all depends.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:40:57): It was kind of a loaded question.", "token_estimate": 329, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:39:41", "end_timestamp": "00:40:57", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "24c571af-a7a3-459d-b67e-7b7388fcb613", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:41:00): It depends on what objectives you're trying to solve for. And the thing that I think a lot of people, no one outside the product development community or the product development body, meaning product, sales, marketing, engineering, all these sorts of things would understand this inherently that part of how you get more traction with a retailer is to have more products that they can sell. If you don't have enough products for them to justify the wall space, they won't put it. That right there is like the left field issue, a left field issue that it has nothing to do with product design per se, or a good strategy on best practices for a product line plan. It has to do with, well, politically speaking, we have to get this much real estate or we don't get anywhere. So we probably need to put three more products in the line just so we can justify that it's a bit of an edge case. (00:42:01): But I'll just start by saying those sorts of things. There are lots of those sorts of things happening that have very little to do with the actual nuts and bolts of design or the nuts and bolts of the strategy. However, just from a pure strategy standpoint, the right answer is that I would advise clients to have the right number of products to support the customer needs and the brand promises and no more, but also no less. So if you have two products that are doing effectively the same thing, what you've created now for your customers is confusion because they can't distinguish between the two and all the marketing and communication and advertising and packaging, all the other things will not ever make that any better because you then have to make arbitrary distinctions that most people intuitively understand our flaw. They can say, well, look, you told me that these are different, but I can see that they're not different. (00:42:53): So other than one being green and one being blue, what's the difference? And some point it's the retail employee at REI that looks in the eyes that says, listen, there one screen and one's blue, like shut up and fly one. You go, I knew it, I knew it, right? But they only are interpreting what they see. So the best solution is that you have the right number for the right solution. And that can be one or many. It depends on what you're selling for a knife brand. So the answer is probably quite a few because we know that customers will buy more than one. The same customer will buy more than one knife. They'll keep coming back for more things. That's great. We want that. We want to give people more options. But even within that two knives that effectively fit the exact same use case, and the only distinction is maybe a, I don't know, a name change, and there's really no a name change in a slight design difference. Well, that feels like redundancy, and it becomes really hard to tell that story. It becomes you're wasting resources and trying to tell a story that can't tell itself at that point. That's silly.", "token_estimate": 749, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:41:00", "end_timestamp": "00:41:00", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "524340c4-b49e-41c3-a97a-44d7d9366aae", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Speaker 3 (00:44:07): So in a more\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:44:12): Use specific case, a snowshoe or a stove or water filter, I think these are things that I'm familiar with. How many water filters are you likely to buy in a decade? One, two, maybe if you had a couple of different use modes that were really important, like large groups versus small groups or solo versus family or something, but they're expensive. They're not the world's most straightforward product to use. And there's a bit of thought that goes into that. So I would argue that it should be very, very clear to the customer which one they should choose. And there should probably be only one, not because we don't want to give people options, but because if you really believe that this is the best way to do a thing, choice A, then why on earth are you going to give them choice B, which isn't quite as good, but then what story are you actually telling people? (00:45:07): Now you've muddled your own storytelling by saying, well, the best way to make pancake is for the battery and the skillet and flip it with a thing. But if you don't like that way, we have this other way which isn't nearly as good. It's half the price and it probably isn't going to give you a very good experience, but try that one. Best of luck. It's also our brand. And now the customer's like, well, how do I even trust that you're telling me the truth? Because you just told me two separate stories. So the simple logic is that if you have a truth that you believe in, which I believe any brand should have, then why are you telling half truth and trying to sell those as the answer, right? I mean, that's a religious conversation at some point. There's the one God, well, if you believe that there's the one, then why tell people there's other ones, right? I mean, that's what it comes down to.", "token_estimate": 461, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:44:07", "end_timestamp": "00:44:12", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "a3a89dfd-b44c-4fc0-9e0b-66ea459cf762", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:45:55): What happens when a company comes to you and they're trying to compete with something else that's come out that's already mind blowing and now they're just trying to catch up. But I'm assuming they come to you and they say, Hey, we see this thing is out there, but we think we can do it better. Does that often happen? Does it turn out better or is playing catch up? Just inevitably, what from the mindset of the leadership on the company needs to be in place in order to actually catch up with a product?", "token_estimate": 133, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:45:55", "end_timestamp": "00:45:55", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "a596e4e1-ed35-4d36-a900-d1c3ba6e595d", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:46:36): So there are companies out there I think, who just want to make, they just want to catch up. They just want to make a thing like that thing because that thing exists and they know they can do it. I'm not interested in working with those people. If the best thing you can do is copy the next guy, go do that, but don't ask me to do it. It's healthy. It's not. It gets into legal gray areas, and I think ultimately it's not, I question your motivation for why you're doing what you're doing. You're just making money at that point. You're just copying somebody else's dream. So we don't need more of that. That's inauthentic to what I think good product is. So the way that it works for the rest of the world is we all know what's out there. We see it, we're aware of it. (00:47:24): And my counsel to most people in that situation is see it, acknowledge it, be mindful of its existence, and then forget about it and go focus on what's important to you. Because two brands with two different philosophies, different values, different promises, will fundamentally solve for the same customer needs in different ways. And while you should know that somebody else has solved it in an interesting way, there are always other ways to solve it that will be more relevant to the way that your brand will solve it. So that pretty readily frees people from that albatross of that other thing being out there. Well, they did that. Yeah, fine. So that's they do. But I'm trying to think of a good analogy here. (00:48:19): There's a way that I'd make a sports analogy if I knew anything about sports, but there's a way that Picasso painted. How many people want to be Picasso? Well, I don't know. I mean, probably a lot of people wanted to be Picasso, but how well do you think you're going to paint like Picasso? Because the only thing you're ever going to do is paint like Picasso. So why would you waste your time? Go be aware of Picasso, learn how he did his work, learn all the ways that he moved, brush around and did sculpture and understood the world, and then forget about it and go do your thing. Be inspired by that. Be motivated by that. But if you're just going to try to be Picasso, you'll forever be compared to that. And on a brand and product development strategy, why on earth would you want your brand to be compared to somebody else's for very long? (00:49:07): The minute that you start talking about somebody else's brand, now all you've done is given all the power and conversation to that brand. If I'm Mercedes and all I talk about is how much better I am than BMW, what is any reasonable minded customer going to do? They're going to go look at BM BMW and say, well, you keep telling me how much better to them. I should go see what they're like first. Oh, they're really good. Well, now Mercedes, or you can actually be better than BMW. I don't even want you thinking about BMW. If my Mercedes, I want you to come over to Mercedes and forget that BMW ever existed. So why on earth would I put anything into the marketplace that had that kind of compare? It would even allow my customers to make the comparison.", "token_estimate": 788, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:36", "end_timestamp": "00:46:36", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "97e9fb6e-04cb-46dc-817e-5b160d137443", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:49:45): Are there instances where two companies who could serve the same audience create almost identical products or they serve a similar purpose? Have you ever seen them join forces and team up in an instance like this where they're no longer competing with each other, but they're somehow working together in this hypothetical scenario?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:50:12): Not yet. I think it probably happens in some digital spaces more often than physical spaces. And my suspicion is partly that\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:50:24): There's a lower cost to\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:50:30): Developing some of that stuff. In terms of building tools, who owns the tool? No, I think the pointy teeth of capitalism tend to keep most companies from collaborating like that. But I've seen you see a lot of collaborations, but it tends not to be people who are overlapping in competitive space. It tends to be people that are mutually leveraging each other's prowess and working to a common cause. The common cause story is that you're trying to provide water for people in Africa, and maybe there's a collaborative effort that happens between two companies because they're both focused on that thing, and it elevates both brands. It allows both brands to make profits and so on. But I think you get, when you talk about sharing intellectual property and all that sort of stuff, and then once the mission's been accomplished, then what? And then where does it go? So no, I have never worked on anything where I've seen that kind of collaboration happen yet, but I think it'd be an interesting opportunity to try to do something like that, but I don't quite know how it would go down.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51:38): Yeah, I guess the one instance that I can think of is here locally, there's been breweries that have done collaborations together where this team of brewers will connect with this team of brewers and they'll make a combined beer. It's a little different though maybe in a disposable format like a beer rather than a product.", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:49:45", "end_timestamp": "00:51:38", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "a52214c4-48ad-429c-95c4-0a179b3f4edf", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:51:59): It's ephemeral, right? I mean, a beer doesn't have to last very long in probably some minutes. And so you can put a run of beer out that elevates both brands, brings awareness to both brands. And the fact is that outside of the Anheuser-Busch, whatever that giant thing is, most small craft brews understand that most beer aficionados drink many different brands of beer. I mean, how many beer craft beer drinkers tend not to be loyal to one brand and only want that. They want to try a lot of stuff. So that collaboration becomes an opportunity to bring in people that only know one brand or the other, to bring awareness to the other brand at no cost to either brand, but only benefit, right? It makes the brand that they knew look better, like, Hey, we're playing nice with these guys. And then it exposes that customer, all this new stuff they didn't know about. Everybody wins. There's no cost, there was no real, and it's gone and it's gone. You can kill that run after a month, and it was a flurry of excitement. Now it's over with in the product world, it's trickier, but you'll see things where one company's technology is embedded in another company's product. Went intel inside of everything. It's one, but didn't really feel like that was very collaborative. That was more of a business relationship. There's a\n\nCole Heilborn (00:53:24): Massive licensing opportunity there.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:53:26): Yeah, I wish more companies would do that kind of thing, but I think it's complex on the business operations end of stuff, frankly.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:53:35): Interesting. What are some cardinal sins that you always stay clear of on strategy or design side? You mentioned one, which is if there's a hole, maybe there's a hole for a reason. What are some others?", "token_estimate": 451, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:59", "end_timestamp": "00:53:35", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "75080112-1d2e-4bab-89f4-adbe97974d64", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Josh Buesseler (00:53:55): There's a book I'm going to mention here that it's aging out, I think, but it's still really relevant and it makes me sad that it's not being updated. It's called the 22 Laws, 22 Mutable Laws of Branding. I'm sure your listeners are familiar with this book, but it's something that I'm surprised more people aren't aware of, but there are some very, very good cardinal rules in there. All of them in fact are quite good. But beyond that, and I don't know if it's in the book or not, but I at least want to mention it. So if somebody says, well, that's in that book, yes, it's one of the things that goes on any whiteboard and quickly in a conversation where I'm talking to a client about trying to figure out what they should be focusing on is that you will be defined by what you say no to. (00:54:44): Yes is easy. No is hard, and what you're willing to walk away from will define more about your brand than anything else because you are now drawing a line in the sand to say, we are not going to get that money. Nothing is more painful for a business than to not get the money. And so I think it's a bold thing, but I think it's true whether you subscribe to it or not, it's true whether you do it or not because at some point you will have to say no. So you don't choose what you say no to. You'll eventually have to say no to things because forced to so better to make the choice and go in that direction. So I think that's pretty strong. I don't know if there are a lot of cardinal rules because really it depends on the situation. I don't even generally know what I'm encountering before I walk in the door. (00:55:33): And there I'm faced with some new challenge. Maybe a more banal cardinal rule is just simply that communication is so imperative inside of companies, and companies think they do it really well and they generally don't. I'm always astonished to find out the two people who sat back to back for 15 years never really talked about what they understood the company's vision and mission to be, or you turn those people around in a meeting and they force them to put their faces together and they realize that the divide was way bigger than they thought it was. They're like, oh, man, you thought it meant that? No, I totally thought it meant this. We've been sitting here grinding away for our entire life force for 20 years, and we never really came to an agreement on what it was we were trying to do. (00:56:22): Most problems within a company that involve a lack of understanding or investment from teams comes from poor communication or misallocated assumptions, misaligned assumptions, things like that. The product design side, I have to edit out two minutes of silence while I scratch my head on this one. This is a really challenging thing to invent in real time here. I don't walk in with the script, so I don't have a formula that I'm approaching anybody with. I think if you don't have somebody in your company that is truly the champion for the product, it's chances for success are very thin. I mean an individual, somebody for whom it is their responsibility to ensure that that thing survives the process of product development, which is brutal. The number of setbacks and failures and reasons why it can't be done, and people that will fight it all the way along is formidable, and it truly takes a person. (00:57:26): The defacto person is often the product manager in the world's most thankless and challenging job. And if those people can survive it, that's good. It doesn't have to be them, but if somebody isn't stewarding that product through the process, it's doomed. It will be a sheer chance that it gets to production. Or you have some machine, some process in your company where things just kind of fall out the back end of the wagon constantly and there's momentum, but they tend not to be that good. So it really takes somebody to push that. Yeah. I'm struggling to come up with great cardinal rules here. If I come up with something else, I interject, but it's a tricky one.", "token_estimate": 1014, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "00:53:55", "end_timestamp": "00:53:55", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "da06b19f-01d5-46ff-b7ba-193b2c1efa99", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:58:04): So if you combine product and values, maybe a loose outcome is brand. Would you agree with that?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:58:13): Interesting. That's one way to look at it. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:58:16): Okay. How would you look at it?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:58:17): No, I think that's right. I think that's right. I think it tends to be thought about from the other direction. Well, we\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:58:23): Have values and that's our brand, and then here's some stuff.\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:58:32): What do you think? Well, yeah, that's a way to do it, but I think the way you're describing is actually right. The way I would describe it is this, and I said it before, your product is the evidence of the values in your brand more than anything else. If you're a product company, what else you doing? But making product, the number of people in a product company that don't seem to be aligned with that is sometimes surprising to me When asked what their role is. It has very little to do with the actual outcome of the process.\n\nSpeaker 3 (00:59:09): So yeah, I think that's the right alignment of things there. Was there a place\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:59:21): You wanted to go with that? Yes.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:23): Yes. I'm curious,\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:59:25): Buttering that bread nicely,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:29): Can those be flip flopped? Can you take a product and then pivot your brand I and then change product? Or can you have a brand change your brand and that influences product? I mean, I'm sure the answer is yes, those things can happen, but how does that play out?\n\nJosh Buesseler (00:59:47): Man, it's unusual. It is usually either, I believe it generally comes about out of necessity or out of, again, chance, luck, something you were doing one thing and it didn't make any sense, but you found this other thing and you jumped on that wagon and off you went. And I'm trying to think of a good example of that. Smith and Wesson used to own the Smith Tower, which is right next door where my office here,\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00:19): Is that where it got its name?", "token_estimate": 526, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "00:58:04", "end_timestamp": "01:00:19", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "b3c53732-393e-451c-95d0-47583be44416", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Josh Buesseler (01:00:21): I'm not actually sure about that. My understanding of history in this area is a little fuzzy and the stories are a little big, but I know that at one point, Smith and Wesson owned the building and at that time, Smith and Wesson made typewriters along with other things, and Smith and Wesson no longer makes typewriters. So something happened in their product offering that shifted dramatically. I have no idea what caused Smith and Wesson stop making typewriters.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00:46): I had no idea.\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:00:47): I don't know if they actually made firearms at the same time as they made typewriters either. So there's a big question there, but there are lots of brands, especially old brands that have survived many, many iterations of, I mean Nintendo, I'm pretty sure it was Nintendo that started out making fertilizer or something that got into video games. I can't remember what it was, but there are a bunch of brands like that that just did one thing and now do a completely different thing. In the modern era, if I had a client say that came to me and said, Hey, we're thinking about going a completely right turn. One thing that is completely unrelated to the other is my question would be, well, your brand didn't go away, so you're going to rewrite your brand. You can just completely reinvent this because then you've just liquidated everything that your brand actually means to people. (01:01:41): And brand values tend not to have much to do with product at all, right? If you look at brand value statements from most companies, you can go online and look up half most of them. Most websites have some little pithy thing in there. They're just these abstract sort of emotional statements or ideological statements. I had a couple here just in case we got into a topic like this. Arc. Arc is founded on the idea that there is always a better way. There's a better way to make a hot dog. Could arx make hot dogs? Well by that brand value? Arguably, yes. Would anybody buy an Arteric hot dog? Maybe not today, but maybe they would eventually. If it was probably\n\nCole Heilborn (01:02:24): Be really high quality,", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:00:21", "end_timestamp": "01:02:24", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "9145195b-92a8-41db-8c35-f70170a28e99", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Josh Buesseler (01:02:26): It would be extremely expensive. We know that for sure, and it would probably taste very, very good. But Patagonia makes food now, right? Patagonia provisions. There's a good example of making a complete left turn. But because Patagonia's brand values are entirely about saving the world, they can smear that sauce on everything. It's really easy for them to pivot. The first few times are going to be really painful. I mean, Patagonia provisions I got to think was like, can we even do this? And they're like, I don't know. Why not? Let's try it. We've got so much money coming in from other places. We can take a complete loss on this and try it, but the next one will be easier and one after that will be even easier and so on until we just understand that Patagonia is all over the place that Patagonia is making stuff to save the world. Who cares about clothes? Clothes were just the wooden arrow to get in, and now they're shooting golden arrows everywhere else. So it's possible. And there's a good example. I've walked myself into a good answer here, finally, Josh. But I think that there's the way to do it. It's incremental. And if you're not bleeding out, losing money on the current thing, in which case you've got a lot of other issues, that's a hail Mary shot. We got to save the business. So let's just try something completely different. (01:03:46): Then the only other right way to do it with a successful business is to put something out there that you can afford to take a complete loss on, try and seek. But what's most fascinating to me is it's still in line with brand values key. So your product is still evidence of what your company is all about, which is why Apple's going to get into cars and VR and AR and whatever other imaginable thing. I mean, Apple's been rumored to get into things like healthcare. Now they're doing finance stuff, they're all over the place. I mean, Apple's basically become its own financing company with Apple Card and all that stuff. So can they do that? Absolutely, because it falls in line with the rest of their brand values, I think for the most part. And it supports the current products that they're selling so fantastic. It's like a win across the board. They're just reinforced and they've increased the breadth and the depth of their brand all at once.", "token_estimate": 586, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "01:02:26", "end_timestamp": "01:02:26", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "33259dea-2289-49fd-bf69-e4f6489b56f0", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:04:43): A couple of years ago, maybe a year and a half ago, Todd Bischoff from Gerber was on the show and he was sharing a story about how Gerber originally was a marketing agency, and they gave out knives as gifts to their clients. And somewhere along the way, the clients were like, these knives are incredible. And I don't know the outcome of their marketing work, but at some point along the way, they decided to pivot and start making knives. And I don't know if that was recent. I think that was 20, 30, 40, maybe 40 years ago. I don't know. But that's one instance that I still kind of blows my mind that those sort of, especially from a service to a product like that, is impressive.", "token_estimate": 178, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:04:43", "end_timestamp": "01:04:43", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "ffabb77f-0e11-42a9-81a4-710efd49af1d", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Josh Buesseler (01:05:26): So there are things that come up for me from time to time that are parallel to this, where a company is doing one, they're doing a couple of different things, and one of them is what they're really focused on, and the other one is actually more successful. And sometimes I see it, it's plain as day, and I pointed it out and they're like, yeah, yeah, but they kind of have this disregard for it somehow. And I go, but your money's over there. And they're like, yeah. And I go, I have to dig into, it's almost like a psychology session of figuring out what their aversion to going in that direction is. And oftentimes you find out that for some reason they felt like it wasn't totally in line with their ideals, or they had hyper focus on something that they were attached to and getting them to let go of that to see that there's a greater opportunity is the thing. (01:06:23): So yeah, I mean, to that point, you're giving away a thing that people really love. Well, maybe you should consider putting price tag on that and making more of them if that's really where people are heading. Generally speaking, I think it's advantageous that you tell your customers where you want to go with your business and you drive it as the business owner. It's your investment, it's your money, it's all that stuff. But sometimes your customers tell you, I think maybe there's a balance of power there and a balance of control. Sometimes it's really important to listen to what your customers are telling you and where they're telling you you should go. A lot of times the customers want to tell you where they think, where they individually think you should go, and it's lost. But the market will tell you. But not always, because sometimes the things aren't in the market. Gerber wasn't really selling knives to say, well, we have sales data on how these knives are doing. No. They're like, well, you just get a lot of people telling us they really like these knives making up the story, but maybe we should consider making product line out of that. Boom. Now they're a knife brand among other things. But yeah.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "01:05:26", "end_timestamp": "01:05:26", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "fe49a861-6446-4ba2-a31b-072508e846a4", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:07:32): Josh, if folks want to follow along and get connected with you and maybe continue this conversation outside of this format between us, where can they go to get connected?\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:07:44): I've been told that I'm rather hard to find online, so I'll say that I have a website. It's not very well updated that is currently in progress. I have a website. We are delicious.com, just like it sounds. Email is also good. It's on my website. You can find it there, but it's just anything. Any name@wearedelicious.com. Those are probably the best places. I don't spend a lot of time prognosticating on social media. I do some podcasts very occasionally, and that's about it. I welcome any point of contact. Email is good enough. I'd be happy to engage with anybody on any conversation for sure. I'm passionate about this stuff and it's really fun to talk about.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08:32): Cool. Well, I thank you for taking the time to chat. Any other thoughts? Anything else you want to leave our audience with before we sign off?", "token_estimate": 265, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "01:07:32", "end_timestamp": "01:08:32", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "f2d6f46b-81ba-4313-9e8e-4a049e46ee1a", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 31, "content": "Josh Buesseler (01:08:41): I think this, I'll say this as a consultant, there are a lot of consultants out there, a lot of people who are very eager to tell you what you want to hear and take your money in the process and if they're giving you good advice. But I think it takes grit to speak truth to somebody that's paying you to say otherwise. And I think it's something, my goal in this business is to leave it better than I found it. That's it. Leave consulting better than I found it. Leave design better than I found it, leave the process of product development better than I found it. And they're all places to improve. And so I want to say this in a way that doesn't put anybody else down or take from somebody else, but I think there are better ways to do a lot of these things. (01:09:39): And there are not a lot of people really professing better ways. So I hope that people can find better ways to do these things and that the process is the most important part of the outcome in some ways. So I don't know, this may not be worth saving in this thing, but something in there I think is meaningful. I don't know. That's the best I got. So my parting comment is I want to see this. I want to see this process improve. I want to see every industry that I touch improve every product that I touch, improve. And the only way to do that is through pushing hard realities to people that they don't always want to hear. And that's the hardest part of my job, is to tell people the thing that they don't want to hear, but they need to hear. Figuring out how to say it, an effective way to get them to listen and shouting. It doesn't work. It's a lot of listening. It's a lot of listening. It could all be better with more listening.", "token_estimate": 434, "speakers": ["Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "01:08:41", "end_timestamp": "01:08:41", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "a06641fd-9345-4d3f-bc07-f877e0fafec5", "episode_id": "b9f9b4f5-5ab9-4160-b49c-d1ecf9661408", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4896", "chunk_index": 32, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:10:40): Yeah. Well, I think if there's one thing that I've taken away from this conversation is that there's a lot more to this than just creating a product, throwing it in a manufacturing line and having it come out the other end. There's a lot of questions, a lot of thought, a lot of nos, and so many roadblocks and so many pitfalls, and it's very clear that your passion for this is obvious. It comes through in the level of intentionality and depth that you think about this stuff. So I appreciate you taking the time to share. This has been a really fun conversation and I look forward to maybe, who knows, maybe having a part two conversation about this when Apple comes out with hopefully the next groundbreaking thing.\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:11:25): Yeah, I look forward to it. It's been real ful. Thanks a lot.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:29): We shouldn't hang our conversation on that though. That\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:11:32): Might. Yeah, let's not do that one.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:34): We might not come back.\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:11:35): Yeah, we'll find something else to talk about, I'm sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:37): All right, well take care, Josh.\n\nJosh Buesseler (01:11:38): Alright, man. Likewise.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:41): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 367, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Josh Buesseler"], "start_timestamp": "01:10:40", "end_timestamp": "01:11:41", "podcast_index": 109, "episode_name": "Ep. 109: Product Design and Brand Development | Josh Buesseler | Delicious, LLC", "slug": "ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-109-product-design-and-brand-development-josh-buesseler-delicious-llc", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Josh Buesseler has been an industrial design and product strategy consultant for 14 years, with clients in many industries including outdoor. Prior to that, he worked in-house at Cascade Designs, which is now also a client along with SOG Knives.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "1d71e13d-a087-4aff-8253-787893e2981f", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Rich Hohne (00:02): COVID was God. It was the best and the worst thing that could have happened to us. I mean, surely financial, it was a disaster.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures. Whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer, I hope you enjoy today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo. Today I'm sitting down with Rich Han, the director of brand and consumer Experience at Ovos Footwear. Rich, how are you?\n\nRich Hohne (00:53): I'm doing well, Cole, great to be with you here today.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:55): Yeah, excited to chat. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nRich Hohne (00:59): I am from my home in downtown Bozeman, Montana, about five blocks from the headquarters of Oboes right here, founded and still here in Bozeman.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08): Okay, right on. So I'm really curious to kind of give a quick intro just on Oboes. Who's the company? What is the company, what do you do?", "token_estimate": 354, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:08", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "21651449-5c1e-4071-866e-828c3fac42a7", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Rich Hohne (01:16): Yeah, so obos is about 13 years old or so. It's a pretty young brand, like I said, started here in Bozeman by some gentlemen who were total shoe dogs in the shoe industry. Those are people that have been in the shoe industry forever or know about all the stuff about biomechanics of the foot and all the different technology for footwear. And they just kind of saw the landscape and thought it wasn't that impressive from a trail shoe perspective. So they started obo. OBO stands for Outside plus Bozeman. It had some other weird names before and now people OBO Obos, but it's outside. Plus Bozeman is how we get our name. And they started it to, before they even had a design or certainly a sample of a shoe, they had already inked major contracts with REI and so they grew this brand and did a great job growing this brand and really service oriented, really just B2B, so really centric on helping retailers understand our footwear, sell footwear in general and pull one of our shoes off when they pulled the other brands off, do this business about a couple of years ago, Capmandu, that company I just talked about who's a retailer and sort of vertical merchandiser, bought Obos and pulled it into their business. (02:40): So we're now owned by Cat Mandu, which is based out of Christchurch, New Zealand. Cat Mandu is subsequently bought a brand called Rip Curl, which is a much larger brand and much older brand than us, and certainly a brand. I've had a bunch on my head and back and wetsuits and things like that, and they're based out of, obviously Australia have a major presence in Southern California as well. So we're part of this triumvirate, this three brand portfolio of Cat Mandu Holdings and it's been awesome. I've been here since about 11 months now, exactly 11 months today on the 9th of December last year. So it's a cool little company. It does extremely well with brands with retailers like REI and Shields, some of the other bigger players, and then a whole network of, I don't know, 700 or so retail doors in the specialty realm.", "token_estimate": 519, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "01:16", "end_timestamp": "01:16", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "54307b83-7b13-4525-9880-f2457b09d947", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:37): Very cool. Yeah, so 13 years ago when the brand was first started, what did the landscape look like and what did the founders see as the opportunity?\n\nRich Hohne (03:46): Well, yeah, certainly. I think the landscape was dominated by a few players. You had Vasts and Garmont, certainly Keen was a huge player. Merrill was a big player and it just felt, I've only had limited conversations with the founders. Unfortunately, John Connolly is the main founder and right when I started he wanted to go out to lunch and tell me his story and stuff because as a marketing guy, you really do tell a lot stories and John used to come into the office all the time. COVID has blown all that up, but they just saw us see a sameness and they saw things that weren't trying to start with the foot, that weren't trying to address how trail footwear in particular was addressing the customer's needs. And that's to comfortable out of the box fit a wide range of foot shapes. They went across the country and did all sorts of using a bran device to get the length and widths of all of thousands of different people. (04:50): It sort of legendary story. And they wanted to create something that could fit a wider range of foot types and be comfortable and durable and also allow people to get after it. And so they said, when you're pulling that Keen and Merrill off the shelf, just bring us along with we really think we're going to compete well when the customer tries on that shoe as well. And they would try the obos on and be like, wow, what is this? I haven't even heard of this oba, what is this? And they're like, no. And we had this great story that we plant a tree for every shoe that we make. And so we got that story to the frontline employees of these retailers and all of a sudden they were telling this, oh, this is this cool niche brand in Bozeman. And retailers really liked us because we didn't go direct. We weren't direct yet. We will be here soon, but we were just all about helping them sell our shoes. And so we became a good partner with a lot of these great retailers, both large and small across the country. We drove around in Vans for years, which we had to shutter obviously because of COVID and did clinics and show people how to sell footwear. And oftentimes, by the way, cool, not just sell our footwear, how to sell footwear in general. (06:05): There's stories, countless stories of our field team sitting in REI with what they call the green vest, your REI that wear green vests, that's what we call 'em, green Vest. And I've seen this person where someone who used to work for me, unfortunately Dave left the company a few months ago to do something else, but he'd be like, hi, man, may I help you? And the woman would be like, yeah, I'm looking at these shoe, oh, have you ever thought about this shoe? And he would go and he'd pull out these three brands. He'd come in here and the green vest would stand back because Dave knew so much about how to fit people with their footwear. Sometimes they'd walk out with another shoe and he'd be like, that's awesome, have a great experience. So it was a real genuine, real Montana centric sort of earnestness that this brand was built on and it's succeeding to this day in spite of everything.", "token_estimate": 820, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "03:37", "end_timestamp": "03:46", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "9a5461e5-1bb0-4b1d-a995-4496b88cd924", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:54): No, that's awesome. And your role with the company as the director of brand and consumer experience, what does that entail?\n\nRich Hohne (07:00): Yeah, so effectively it's anything that the customer sees or touches or interfaces with the brand is under sort of my purview. So that's everything from typical table stakes like advertising and website content to pr, social, all the events, all the partnerships, all the retail marketing, meaning what does the brand look like at retail and what type of things can you help retailers with that can help them sell our shoes to the field and education component, which we have an outreach team of two. We have two vans. One's in Dallas has been stuck there since COVID. The other one's here in Bozeman and Sprinter vans, they drive around normally and do in-person trainings. We've moved that all to the virtual platforms, so all of that as well. And then I interface really closely with the product team on sort of how are we designing shoes to match the customers that we want to attract or retain. So that's been really fun, especially going to a smaller brand. I think we have 27, 28 people now, so it's very manageable. But basically those are the type of things. There's probably a couple of different things I'm missing, but that's about it.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:19): Oh, interesting. Expand on what you just said there. You said that you guys are trying to design shoes to attract and keep certain types of customers.", "token_estimate": 373, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "06:54", "end_timestamp": "08:19", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "7c30f9e0-eb7b-481e-8daa-e23937109304", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Rich Hohne (08:28): So the secret sauce in marketing in general is sort of knowing who you're talking to and then talking to them in hopefully an effective manner. So what happens oftentimes though in brands, and I haven't seen a ton of this at Obos, but I certainly have other brands, is product people know how to build product they build, they're usually very, very good at it. Oftentimes they build it for themselves or people they know or out of almost intuition because they have a sense of the marketplace. (09:04): But really what you need to do is sort of put the consumer centric and you're not the consumer, first of all, you don't pay retail if you get things for free because you, I don't thankfully pay for many shoes. So you really need to put the customer there and that research and that effort can be painstaking to find out not only who your customer is, but is that going to match your business needs going forward. If you find out your customers is an 85-year-old man or woman, I'm sorry, that doesn't have legs, right? It's not to be insensitive, it's just they're going to age out of the activity. Are you seeing in the data other consumer profiles through segmentation work that you can sort of then turn some attention towards? And then because then you have someone in mind when you're doing things either building product or advertising campaigns or content strategies or retail POP or any of that, and those kind of have to, it's easier so than done. Those sort of have to match in order to be optimally effective.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:13): So that's something that I've heard a lot of marketers say is we're talking about who they're marketing to, and oftentimes they'll say something like, well, our marketing team is the consumer, so we just kind of ask ourselves what would we want to see, what we want to hear? So I'm curious for you, what's that balance look like as you're considering your own personal perspective as you're doing your job, but how are you balancing that with your consumers that you've identified? Are you one of the consumers?", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:28", "end_timestamp": "10:13", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "c9e190e0-2e0f-4e6f-939c-6b3682dbf494", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Rich Hohne (10:45): It's interesting. I probably am, but not, no, actually I don't think I am actually. You obviously want to build, you want to sell stuff or market stuff or support stuff through operations or service things through customer service that you like that sort of resonate with you. So you are always part of that feedback loop. But really what you need to do to be most effective is to put yourself into consumer's shoes and say, okay, well if you're trying to build for a millennial audience, say 20, whatever that is, 25 to 38 or 39, I don't remember the exact dates, it changes every year. So such a bright statement, but you've got to put yourself in their shoes because if you're not, no one is exactly that person, especially when you're in the brand and you're in the four walls, unless you're selling marketing software, marketing teams aren't really the answer or the audience. So I always try to distance myself and either talk to people that are the audience or try to put myself in the shoes of those key audience segments.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:07): Okay. So backing way up, but you said the founders kind of went on this cross country road trip to fit a whole bunch of feet sizes. Was that a large data gathering process of finding out who your customer is?", "token_estimate": 321, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:45", "end_timestamp": "12:07", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "522027bd-22ee-448c-b3e0-aed6d260d66f", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Rich Hohne (12:23): Yeah, it was. I mean the legend is, and I've never heard this straight from the mouse, people legends are oftentimes way better than the truth. So the legend is, yeah, on taking branded devices, branded devices are those sliding things that you see in the stores and metal devices that sort of look like some sort of middle-aged torture device for your foot and took that to airports and took that to a consumer event and trade shows, they just drug it along everywhere they went from the notion I get and they just took data and they said, okay, and you end up with this sort of OBO foot that's not necessarily revolutionary, but it does have a simple goal of trying to fit more types of shoe sizes or foot sizes out of the gate. It's got the narrow heel, so it really kind of holds you in, locks you in when you're on the trail. (13:16): It's got a little bit of a wider tow box to support a little bit more width. I mean we're seeing even wider profiles come out and it gives people maybe get heavier or understand that their wides, but still the ability to hug with the design of the textiles, working with the interfacing with the last of the shoe, the ability to hug that middle of your foot over the arch of your foot there. And also we have a really good insole that comes stock in our shoes that has really good support and cushion as well. And then we have a sort of a heightened version of our insole as well that can provide you an additional comfort and they're really, really good insoles. And so people, that was another driving point is they said, man, these insoles feel great and they were putting it in their other shoes and taking 'em out and then all of a sudden we knew we had something.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:14): I'm curious to hear a little bit more about your background and you said you've only been with the company now for 11 months. What's the prior last few years looked like?", "token_estimate": 481, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:23", "end_timestamp": "14:14", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "a223de6e-24b0-4fee-b273-7c7229e7cb89", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Rich Hohne (14:24): Last few years. Wow. I moved to Montana in 1994. I got out of college, I was a history and German major and just decided I wanted to come up to Montana. I was in Los Angeles, California where I grew up and wanted to just be somewhere different for a little bit of time. And 20 some odd years later, I'm still here, but it's been a good summer. But I started my marketing career, probably started in the early two thousands when I started to work in the sort of resort world up in Big Sky Montana, which is about an hour, hour and 15 minutes south of where I am right now. Big Sky was this burgeoning sort of up and coming resort town and amazing Ski Hill kind of never really matured as a town. I was pretty new, started in the seventies, almost like a veil kind of scenario.\n\nSpeaker 3 (15:08): And\n\nRich Hohne (15:09): Then I worked there. We actually, the company I worked for working with the two partners there, we started our own ski resort in 2003\n\nSpeaker 3 (15:18): And", "token_estimate": 250, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "14:24", "end_timestamp": "15:18", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "343368c0-9d07-44cc-92f0-6baed3c3a06e", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Rich Hohne (15:18): That's kind of where I really had to, okay, the marketing and sales is going to be your deal. And it was a pretty big operating to operation to get that going and did that for about four more years. So I was total up in Big Sky about seven years working, amazing experience, really cool, still treasure those days. And then I took about a year off, did some consulting from the house, and then I started working for a company all a while. I used to be a fishing guy in the nineties. I worked at fly fishing and retail. Fly fishing was always my jam and it still is, but I found out that Sims Fishing Products, which is beloved fly angling brand here and based in Bozeman was looking for a marketing manager at the time and I said, I don't how to do that. So I did that and I was there for 11 years and did pretty much about everything I can think of in the marketing world. (16:11): There had some amazing experiences, was part of teams that put together really cool campaigns and really good strategy for the brand and it was an awesome time. And then about a year, about 14 months ago or so, I was tired. I just was like, I kind want a new inbox. I don't like checking this one anymore. So I broke up with the company and it was a tough decision. I talked to a lot of folks that I worked with or worked for me or I worked for still to this day, a lot of great friends there. But I was looking for a new challenge and I had been talking to OBO for a couple months there just to see what was going on with this brand and Bozeman, I had a few friends that I really respected that worked here and ended up working out. So I've been very fortunate to be with some really kind of prominent local brands here in the southwest Montana region and it's allowed me to do a lot of really fun things and meet some amazing people. And really I love being sort of local and working on local brands and those types of things. Not that I wouldn't, they're all brands that aspire to be international and are in some instances, but being based here and sort of the DNA being based here and the culture being based here has always been attracted to me.", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "15:18", "end_timestamp": "15:18", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "d827bc5a-0d9b-4052-9b50-e7e1b681fe8e", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:38): Yeah, okay. So you can't just say, I helped start a ski resort and then just kind of gloss over that and keep moving. I think you need to give us a little more detail on that story. So were you doing sales and marketing prior? How did you get involved with the ski startup, ski hill startup? Tell us that story.\n\nRich Hohne (17:59): Yeah, so this is an interesting story. So in 2000, I started working for an organization called Moonlight Basin Ranch, and it was a big landholder right next to Big Sky Resort. They bought this 25,000 acre parcel about eight years before, had sold off massive chunks of it to conservation buyers in order to get their investment back, set aside this certain part of portion of this land in adjacent to Big Sky in order to have ski and ski out communities like you see in a lot of Western, a lot of resort towns. (18:32): And I started working there in 2000 and it was just trying to figure out, they build a certain number of units every year and then build a certain number of units, wildly successful run and owned by two local gentlemen that are, one unfortunately was a big mentor, mine passed away a few years ago. The other one I still really enjoy seeing and hanging out with and consider him a good friend. And we had this, it was a really successful slow going development company. They had two lifts they had put in the nineties, big Sky operated them, big sky resort, and then it was under the 99 year agreement that we effectively gave them the lift, said, we'll put the lift in, you run it, but they're skiing, ski out access. So we knew we had to move sort of around the corner on this mountain. (19:23): Big Sky has these massive every direction sort of big cir followed by these big arms that sort of stretch out. So we were going to be over kind of the continental divide kind of thing where you couldn't ski back to the current development. So we were like, we're going to put in a big six pack high speed lift. So we had more access to ski and scout terrain. Basic guy was like, whoa, are you going to put in a six pack lift? It was the biggest lift in Montana at the time. We did that. We bought it rather. We said, yeah, yeah, what do you think We're going to put this in, you want to work on, maybe we sell some tickets down there, we do a rev share, we do something. Maybe you can pay us to a management fee to have this lift under your lift tickets. And they went, nah, we don't really need to train. And they just kind of didn't want to make a deal with us and because they didn't think we would do what we eventually did, which was start a steal scope. I remember I went through all negotiations with the two partners. I was the GM of the business at the time, and I remember they called me into their office and they said, alright, rich, here's the deal. And they said, what do you think about starting a ski resort? And I remember going,", "token_estimate": 731, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "17:38", "end_timestamp": "17:59", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "d26a2c46-80c6-42b6-b524-708c00dfe737", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Speaker 3 (20:30): Yeah,\n\nRich Hohne (20:31): Hell yeah, let's start a ski hill. I'd be awesome. Hell yeah, screw those guys. We don't need 'em. We'll just do our own thing. That's easy, right? And I remember going back to my desk after high fiving and all this stuff and sitting down and being like, all we don't know how to start a ski. I ski, but these guys don't even ski. They barely ski. They're ranchers from Ennis, Montana and Enni is an 800 person town in southwestern Montana, ranching and fishing town. I was like, man, I don't know what that we're going to do. So fast forward, that was March of 2003 May, we hired a gentleman to be the GM of the ski hill and Lee and Joe, the owners at the time said, alright, reg, you need to go up and start working in Big Sky every day and run sales and marketing and be the assistant general manager. (21:21): The ski hill, they really trusted me. They knew I didn't know that much, but they at least knew they trusted me and I could go up there, I could be a spokesman for the resort, all that stuff. So I did that and we hired a skeleton crew of amazing people who I still see today to open that up on Thanksgiving of 2003, we had very limited terrain. We put another lift in and then we expanded it with additional lifts over the next several years. And then I did everything there from pr, marketing, sales, did a lot of community outreach stuff and it was amazing experience. It was very, very hard. It was literally tiger by the tail. It was a bunch of people just running and you had a deadline, you had to be open. So I remember, I don't think we actually ended up hitting, maybe it was the day after Thanksgiving, we actually did end up hitting our deadline, but I remember being on Thanksgiving, being there installing, scanning technology for lift tickets and stuff and just trying to make sure it all worked. (22:22): You could literally go up and scan a barcode and it would work. We were in a hole physically. We had to get repeaters bouncing around data and stuff and it was like, I don't, I'm sitting out there going, what am I doing? I don't know what I'm doing. But we hired smart people that did and it was an awesome experience. I still attribute a lot of my luckiness and breaks to that opportunity. I met a ton of people in the community. I got really active in the community up in Big Sky and in Bozeman, and it just was a high profile things everybody knew about this deal trying to change the way we did things are trying to change the way the ski resort operated and the whole industry because we saw that there were opportunities to make it higher touch community service or customer service, excuse me, to be a lot more friendly to locals to sort of just be kind and say thank you. (23:16): I remember one guy came up and he said, man, I really like what all those locals do when they park at the Moonlight Lodge. We had this beautiful lodge they lodge thing. I said, what are you talking about? He's like, yeah, when you get there and everybody flips up their windshield wipers. I went, no, no, no. The staff does that. If it starts to snow, they'll go through all the cars and lift up the windshield wipers. He was like, get out of here. I'm like, God, they're bored. Anyways, they're sitting in the parking lot. They don't have anything to do. We would hand out hand warmers. We were on the north side of the mountain and it was cold. We just sort of thought back to the original or what we talked about a little bit ago. We just thought what are our limitations and what can help them have a better day?", "token_estimate": 897, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "20:30", "end_timestamp": "20:31", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "6ff8318a-c03b-4131-aec8-4083f47e0a46", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:55): Not many people can say they've helped start a ski resort. So taking all of the experience that you learned from the ski resort to Sims, how does that apply to what you guys are working through at Obos currently? Does it apply?\n\nRich Hohne (24:13): I think everything does. That's an interesting question. I think one of the constants that I've sort of discovered is be true to who you are, but that's both personally. And I always tell people gold, don't identify yourself with the people that you work for. Don't be the brand. And that's absolutely what I always am. I just dive in and I'm like, I want to live it and I want to wear the hat and blah, blah, blah, blah and all this stuff because I just kind of getting in there and gna getting to the marrow of things. But I think the constant thing is be authentic to who you are, both as a person and as a brand and as a brand. It's a lot harder because you've got to figure out who you are. (25:05): Montana has, just in all these instances, I've always tried to leverage the authenticity of this state and the authenticity of this state's not the new 78 million home being built wherever, which is more common than it used to be, certainly. But it's the fact, if you're on a rural road, which is easy to find here in Montana and your car breaks down and you walk to the nearest house, they'll probably let you stay there. They'll probably feed you dinner, they'll probably get you help however they need. There's a real kindness and I think approachability of people that live and work in Montana or maybe, and I've always sort of tried to both identify with that personally and how I treat people and then also how the brands operate because those are universal ideals and I don't think that there's, it's not secret sauce. It's actually simple sauce. And then as you're talking about how it is in a business, figuring out what your tone is and what resonates with people and doing more of that. And that's easier said than done because that's a hard paradigm oftentimes to crack. But it's sort of been the common thread, at least in my career. And I know other people that I've talked to that I've learned from have spoken in similar manners and marketing is about, it's about X's and O's, but a lot of it's about emotional chords and emotional impact that you have on people's lives.", "token_estimate": 591, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "23:55", "end_timestamp": "24:13", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "ba28bfa7-b4be-4dbd-b1a7-e7207bd59f73", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:47): Yeah. So looking at the current landscape, the current climate at Oboes, I know as you mentioned, you guys were pretty heavily direct to retail. How has COVID affected that and what are you guys working through currently in response to that?\n\nRich Hohne (27:06): Yeah, COVID was God. It was the best and the worst thing that could have happened to us. I mean, purely financial. It was a disaster because so much of our business, all of our business rather, was to retail doors. Whether big retail doors\n\nCole Heilborn (27:30): Are you a hundred percent to retail.", "token_estimate": 147, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "26:47", "end_timestamp": "27:30", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "a19a2657-d8e6-4005-bff2-8aa1b11e7661", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Rich Hohne (27:32): We are now, yes, and I'll talk about how that's shifting because we have no, we've always sold against other brands that have D two C channels. We said, we're going to be your brand, the retailer. But what happened was March 15th or 13th or whatever, up until mid-June, we didn't have, there was some way people could find our shoes at retailers websites and things like that. But we're a young brand. A lot of people don't know the name Obos when they go in and they see it and they go, what the heck is that? So we've always done a really good job, like I said before, of getting to the people that either own the business or manage the store or are on the floor, what we call kids on the floor, the green vests or whatever, to be our advocates and teach them the story and have them say, wow, that's cool. (28:23): I can tell that story. It's like, yeah, it's a pretty simple story. And that all was gone and we lost all of our, what we kind of thought was our secret sauce of really good service. Now, we did treat our retailers incredibly well. We didn't go off price, we didn't just go, we're just going to say everything's half off just so they can get a little bit of money and we can get additional orders. We maintained the price point of our shoes because we didn't want brand erosion. We didn't want brand customers to get accustomed to buying our shoes at discount. Some of our competitors are very good at the discounting channel and we didn't want to be that brand. So we kept our pricing where it should be. But it was lean and very lean and we just didn't operate a company that could work any other way. (29:13): We were small, we were 25 people at the time or whatever. And so we didn't have ways to do training virtually. We didn't have obviously our own direct to consumer channel. We didn't know what the hell to do, but we did everything we could. We really turned down all operating expenses and just sort of spent only critical funds. We took pay cuts, we did all sorts of things. No one lost a job, which is great. And then as we kind of started to thaw out the world, wanted to get the hell outside as we all do. Oh my gosh. We were just all sort of bummed at staring at our computers all the time. So the whole world wanted to get outside, but the sales have been amazing since that time, but it really made us reevaluate how we're going to do things. We had to do a sales meeting and I sort of took that over and turned it into a brand summit and basically that's when we were launching our spring 21 line. (30:13): It's about to hit the stores here in a couple months, and we had to do that all digitally, and that was hard. I mean, it sounds super easy now that everybody's accustomed to Zoom world and all this stuff, but storytelling, staring at a computer screen is hard. And so how do you do that? Showing footwear, showing how things look and the texture of something and why it's so cool hard, they get samples, but it was hard to get samples as well. So we had to learn. We had to take people off the road that were out there doing in-person training. Our rep force couldn't go into retailers to service them because even when they did open up, they didn't want a bunch of people that weren't buying in there because they had certain requirements of how many people could be in their store. So we had to just absolutely learn how to do a lot of things. It's kind of breaking your right hand and learning how to write. It was really challenging. One thing that our parent company helped us do, they said, okay, I think it's time to look at a direct channel. We were already looking at it. That would be roughly the same timeframe as it'll end up what it'll be, (31:19): Which will be Q1 of next year. But we need to have that because the customer couldn't find our shoes, they couldn't find, we have a robust website that tells you where you can find them, but they couldn't. If they wanted to buy 'em, there was no opportunity for them. So (31:37): We're now spinning that up. We're trying to figure out how we can support our sales rep force out there in the marketplace because we know that they're not able to just go to retail all the time. And so they need new tools in order to sell virtually, which we've developed for them. It's been a whole new reset and a whole dawn of a whole new day. I think they're tools that ultimately we're going to be better for having figured out and discovered and not mastered, but we understand it certainly a lot better than we did before, (32:10): And I think that's going to serve us well going forward. And I think most brands are probably saying that right now, but it was a dark day for a little while. We worked hard and we also just said, let's be kind to our retailers. They're really in a tough spot. Reach out to them, ask 'em what you can do. We made sure all of our retailers were up on our website and as many as we could rather using a piece of software called locally.com, which basically says you can buy this size 10 at these retailers in your local neighborhood. So we wanted to get people spun up like that. So we needed to be creative on how we could still support the critical retail channel. We had no way to get shoes out, and then obviously there was production slowdowns because of COVID and things like that.", "token_estimate": 1333, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "27:32", "end_timestamp": "27:32", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "992b972f-9680-441a-b0e7-cb198da0a0a7", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:56): Yeah, interesting. So a bunch of questions. One of them, where are you at now in the process of opening up direct to consumer channels?\n\nRich Hohne (33:06): So we're building it out right now, so we'll be live Q1 of next year. We're working with a technology partner based out of Whitefish, Montana that we're really psyched to be with. I'm hiring people on the team here to help run that business. So we're just trying to figure that out. And it'll be February, March of next year and it's going to be exciting. We're not going to be this buy it here at all costs from us. We actually think the benefit of going and getting someone to measure your feet and sit down and figure out your width and what most people have different sized feet, which they don't understand, but trying to figure out what style of shoe is the best style for you to be in? Are you looking for just like an urban interface trails around or concrete trails or whatever? Or are you going to go on, you do the Pacific Crest trail or what are you going to do? There's a super large value to going into retail and sitting, you'll get great attention and they will understand your foot. But a lot of times consumers are busy and they want to just get it when they want to get it, or they've had a pair of oboes before and they know their size or they think they do. (34:28): So we think we can drive a brand heavy retail environment, which helps get the word out more about this brand in Bozeman, Montana called obos. That'll ultimately help our retail channel. People will go in understanding what this brand is\n\nCole Heilborn (34:47): Totally\n\nRich Hohne (34:47): And what it means.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:49): So how do you do that? How do you take a brand that people aren't sure how to pronounce and make them familiar with it and they get it? How do you do that?", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "32:56", "end_timestamp": "34:49", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "d39c56aa-6382-4bed-b291-a5dfee30a1ae", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Rich Hohne (35:01): It's tough. The first thing you've got to realize is sort of like you got to realize who you are, and that's a big huge discussion. But effectively it starts with who you think your customer is. It starts with what your product is, and it also starts with what you stand for. At obos, we say we're true to the trail, right? That's our tagline or whatever you call our positioning statement. And true to the trail is an interesting notion because what originally started out was we make a better hiking shoe for the trail, and now what it's morphing is we make footwear for wherever the hell your trail's going. It could be trail to whatever, it could be trail to starting a podcast, it could be trail to do the Pacific Crest Trail, but everybody's on a trail. So you need to figure out what the DNA of your brand is and where you want it to go and what you can back up. So it's not like, yeah, we're the company that makes shoes and Chevy trucks. No, you're not. You can't back that up. And you want to have a sort of selling proposition that is authentic to who you think wants to buy your shoes. (36:17): And that's all easier said than done because brands, it depends. You get different marketing leads or you get different sales leads or CEOs or presidents and everybody wants to move that a lot of times and it morphs just do. It doesn't mean the same thing now that it did in 1985, (36:37): But it's still core. The core is still there, which is awesome. That's why it's such an amazing statement. So you learn, you need to discover the language and the visuals and the sort of identity of your brand, and then you got to hit it hard and you got to target the right people out there, which you can do with an extremely much easier now than you used to be able to. Before it was 15 years ago, it was like buy an ad in this magazine, buy an ad in that magazine, put up a website. But you hope you can find people now you can get very targeted about getting to people at the right time and in the journey of looking for footwear and (37:23): Send a compelling message. That's true, that is honest that, and you hope it resonates with people. We think it does and we think it's powerful and I think you're going to see a lot more out of this brand speaking around those terms of true to the trail and what that exact means for us and could mean for people. But things like truth and things like that are so important. They're kind of universal sentiments, but they feel almost more important now. And that's not necessarily a political statement. It just feels like COVID and all the heaviness going on in our country, it feels like people are just looking for bearing points and we think that that's an opportunity to speak to some of this language.", "token_estimate": 693, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "35:01", "end_timestamp": "35:01", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "c7db073e-d99e-4f8c-b187-b9094f0f4d93", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:06): So expand on that a little bit. What are some of the truths that you're speaking? How are you speaking them?\n\nRich Hohne (38:13): Well, we talk about it and we have this thing that's not really necessarily consumer facing, but you'll see more parts of it perhaps in the consumer facing. It's this compass, and I have a compass somewhere here in my office. But anyways, it's true to the trail. So we say it's true to your foot. So true to your foot is that's a huge part of our DNA. We understand biomechanics. We understand how a pair of shoes can help serve your experience, that we know how to build really, really good shoes and we do. We're not always the leading in technology or coming up with this crazy thing that no one's ever seen, but we're going to make you a really good pair of shoes that you're going to really love spending time in. So true your foot true to our people. So that's both us internally at Oboes. (39:03): It's our one rung out, which would be our parent company and sister companies and our sales force and those types of things. Treating 'em with respect that they deserve, being kind to them, being thoughtful to them. I really believe in that. I think morale is the number one killer of brands and the number one builder of brands that people are stoked to go to work, that they feel respected. It's been a really hard time to manage people during COVID. I've really, really struggled with it, but I think we talked to the team, I've tried to be respectful at least as sort of table stakes and we certainly believe that at Obos, the third part of this compass is true to the experience. So we believe in the experience of getting outside. You can't be a outdoor shoe brand and not 100% believe that the power of the outdoors and it is transformative and it is been transformative for us. (40:05): We work with groups where it has transformed their memberships, whether it is at risk youth organizations or black Americans that aren't really getting access to the outdoors that we think and they think that they should or other maybe underserved parts of our world that don't get exposed to it. And if you are exposed to the outdoors and get your feet on the trail, you're going to be a better human. And so yeah, so true to your experience. I love that part. It's like tell cool stories. You can tell adventure stories and the adventure could be going down to the store or walking down to your buddy's house a mile away, or it could be something much more agro, but there is definitely an experience. And then finally, and these aren't necessarily in any sort of order compass, it goes around the compass. It's true to your community. (40:56): And this is something I think I've talked a little bit about already, but I talked local community that is our outdoor industry community and making sure you're part of things, that you're not just a bystander and that you are getting involved in things you care about, what happens in your local community or your national or international community, whatever it is. And you advocate for things that you think are important. And that's a really important thing to me. It's been important for me really for the past 20 years and didn't always used to be, I didn't really get that. I just say whatever, I'm sure those folks will be okay. No, not really. They're going to need some help. So being true to your community is get involved, be advocates for the things that you want to stand up for and don't just sell shoes. That's the old school business model is I make widgets and sell widgets. (41:57): No, you don't. You are really a trust agent with the consumer and they're going to trust people that are involved and that really kind of care like they do about their community. So those are sort of the four points as we think about what being true to the trail means. What we're trying to figure out now, Cole, is how do you, what's sort of the strategy in order to communicate all those things? They're all great notions, but then you've got to have, you've got a visual identity, copy, identity, all of those things in order to support content strategies to support this and bring it to life. So that's what we're doing right now.", "token_estimate": 1059, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "38:06", "end_timestamp": "38:13", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "de9c88c4-a0f0-4dfc-b536-9231a4a79f31", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (42:40): Yeah, man. What fun. It's\n\nRich Hohne (42:42): Fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:44): Yeah. Anything you can share in terms of specifics, how you're trying to get those four points out?\n\nRich Hohne (42:50): No, I think what you'll see is no, I think the thing that you'll see first and is oftentimes the easiest is either the true to your community, which is we've done a lot of work with local organizations and regional organizations that we've already talked about, like at-risk youth organizations like these Sky Youth Empowerment or Gallin Valley Land Trust, which does a great job of conserving critical corridors for humans and wildlife and things like that in a ever expanding and growing community of Bozeman, Montana and the Gallatin Valley. And then the experience ones is sort of trying to figure out the right people to identify yourself with. There's a lot of influencers. We have our whole influencer group called the Altruists or the Truist program, excuse me, that it's a really cool organization where we bring people in and we say, we want you to be advocates for Oboes and help us get the word out. (43:46): And that's growing and that's exciting. And then you'll start to see as we go to direct where we're able to have more of those one-to-one relationships with our customer. Because right now we don't have a customer. Our customers are our retailers and that's built this business and been amazing. But one thing you lose when you have a business like that is you don't, back to the point originally we spoke about is like you're building things for human beings. And if you don't know or talk to the human being ever, that's hard. Now social media allows you to do that or email, whatever, but it's been tough for us to get that one-on-one relationship. So you'll start to see us build more of that out and hopefully be thoughtful in the manner in which we do.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:22): Yeah, no, that's cool. Okay, so one of my last questions we're coming up at the end of our time for today. What are the biggest right now that you guys face the company as a whole or you and your role, and what are the biggest opportunities that you see and are excited about?", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "42:40", "end_timestamp": "44:22", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "a37b2a0f-6bae-44c0-9e3a-05892a6408f3", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Rich Hohne (44:42): I'd say the biggest challenge is probably is it's a small team trying to do a lot, which is awesome, which is what the kind of environment I've always sort of gravitated towards. I like small teams, but we have a really, really bright future ahead of us. So the challenge is what you take on and what you don't, where do you say no, no is as good option as Yes. So that's never as fun to say, right?\n\nCole Heilborn (45:15): It depends what you're saying no to because there can be some things that feel really good.", "token_estimate": 134, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "44:42", "end_timestamp": "45:15", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "79842a2d-4779-4792-9fb2-150b8dd1c764", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Rich Hohne (45:18): Yeah, no to that massive headache. You're like, yeah, sure, I don't want that. But in marketing it's like, yes, I want you jump at things, right? So having that discipline is tough. And the hardest thing though, without a doubt, is trying to be a leader in times of COVID because you just can't sit down with people, you can't read their temperature, you can't read how they're feeling about things on the team. You can't get a sense until way later if they're stoked to work on something or not. It's just all much more impersonal. That's really been hard on me. I'm a big rah rah team guy, but a lot of that's because I'm in person and (45:58): I'm the loud guy in the office. But I like that comradery and that's challenging with COVID. The biggest opportunity for us, I think is, which gets me super excited about, is building all of these relationships with these customers and really helping be an advocate for their feet and getting them on these great experiences with our footwear and learning more about them and all that. And sending 'em to retailers because they have an awesome event going on. Or they should be the one that sits down with the branded device and helps them find the right size of obos. But just having more opportunities for that. And that's kind of a sea change for us right now and going to be really fun. And then to ultimately see more people in our shoes and to have more people understand how to pronounce our name will be great. (46:57): But Guy, the company is doing super well, especially in these last four months, and it's been a pleasure to join the team and a lot of great humans. It's just at times of COVID, it just makes everything a little bit more difficult and your expectations don't change because you always want to be super sort of go-getter on the expectations. But we've got a big runway ahead of us, of a bright future, and our parent company's awesome who helps us out as much, as much as we need them to frankly. And I don't expect that to ever change and love them. And so I'm just excited. I've had pretty long 10 years with my other job, so I feel like I'm just getting started. And certainly with COVID, I feel like I've been here about three weeks, but almost a year now, Paul, so I'm getting over the", "token_estimate": 571, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "45:18", "end_timestamp": "45:18", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "6415c567-65f7-4212-b9b2-d3f3b0d1b2de", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (47:48): Hill. You are. That's awesome. No, that's cool. I'm excited. You said at the beginning, COVID was the best thing and the worst thing that happened to us. And it's cool to see now that we're kind of past the initial shock in the initial just confusion. I think it's cool now to see what brands like yours are doing now that the landscape has changed and maybe there's other, everyone's kind of looking at their business saying, what are some other ways we can grow? How can we be a little stronger but not lose track of where we've come from and who we are and our values?\n\nRich Hohne (48:30): Yeah, and I'm just so excited. We're building all this cool stuff. We'll have digital platform and D two C sales. We're really good in the room when we get in the room and we talk to people about footwear. And once we can, I feel like that's our gasoline sitting in our can at our feet once we can maybe get past this COVID until the world opens up enough. But we're then driving around again, and I have two people on my team that are amazing humans, Peter and Christina, that want to get back out on the trail in the highways, in the oboes vans and tell people about footwear. If we can have all these digital tools and this digital presence and then come in with this awesome customer service and really personal attention that this brand has really been known for, I just think that's going to make us so much stronger. The waiting game is like, Ugh, we just don't know. We know if, is it six weeks or six months or a year and a half? They just dunno\n\nCole Heilborn (49:21): Being patient, man, that's the toughest part.\n\nRich Hohne (49:24): Well, that's not my forte.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:27): No, you're the type of guy who just says, yeah, let's start a ski hill. Let's do it. Let's start a\n\nRich Hohne (49:31): Skill. Why not? Let's go do it. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:34): Cool, man. Well thank you again for taking the time to chat. If folks want to follow along with you with the brands, where can they go to connect?", "token_estimate": 511, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rich Hohne"], "start_timestamp": "47:48", "end_timestamp": "49:34", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "8d4ad7e0-cbc7-49b2-b085-7543ad16bd2f", "episode_id": "f2d2ffab-1276-4ad1-9848-c1488b11a689", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4851", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Rich Hohne (49:42): They can reach out to me, they can email me and say hi or whatever. That's rich at obos footwear, obo z footwear.com, and then they can see us across all sorts of digital platforms and all that stuff. And I'd love to hear from anybody. If you want to hear more about our brand or have questions about our footwear or just want to say hi.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:01): Sounds good. And if you're in Bozeman, maybe stop on by and go on a run.\n\nRich Hohne (50:06): Stop on by and we'll take you out to see some really cool stuff. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:10): Alright, rich, well thank you again and have a good rest of your day.\n\nRich Hohne (50:12): Thanks Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:17): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 228, "speakers": ["Rich Hohne", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "49:42", "end_timestamp": "50:17", "podcast_index": 46, "episode_name": "Ep: 46 Rich Hohne | True to the Trail | Oboz Footwear", "slug": "ep-46-rich-hohne", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-46-rich-hohne", "guest_name": "Rich Hohne", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Oboz Footwear", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adaptation and e-commerce", "podcast_length": "44:68"}, {"id": "b09332b7-4882-4b15-9a5f-0215de084ec6", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:01): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Chris Pew, the CEO and co-founder from TREW Gear. Chris, welcome.\n\nChris Pew (00:09): What's up Cole? Thanks for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:11): Yeah, thanks for joining. I'm excited to have you on the show and talk all about your TREW to You film series that you guys got going on. Yeah, I love what you guys are doing. I love the creativity and the outside the box thinking when it comes to the big question of as brands, how do we stand out in 2024? Because that's a tough thing to tackle, it seems like. So Chris, I'm curious to hear from you, on a scale of one to 10, how hard is it for brands to stand out in 2024 from your perspective as a business owner?", "token_estimate": 191, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "00:01", "end_timestamp": "00:11", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "9df65b96-e34a-4d55-bcfa-a273795d4908", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Chris Pew (00:50): Well, first of all, thanks Cole for the opportunity to talk about TREW to you. I'm really psyched to be on the show. I love the podcast. I love the conversations you've had exploring the nuances for marketing outdoor outdoor industry. Scale of one to 10, how hard is it to stand out? That is such a hard question to answer. In a strange brand, we're like a legacy small brand. We've been around for 16 years, and so for customers who engage in our particular style of back country skiing and snowboarding, we're pretty well known. But for skiers and snowboarders outside of that kind of core group, we're an anomaly. They probably haven't heard of us. They might recognize the thumbs up or something like that. So I would say if I was starting a brand right now, I would max out that scale that's a 10 because there's so much product out there, there's so much incredible product, there's so much incredible media in content. (01:53): But for us, I think it's a little bit more nuanced in the, I don't know, six or seven range. We have our little slice of the pie that we kind of defend, like our positioning in the industry, and then our strategy for standing out is really about trying to get more people to see what we're doing that we think would appreciate it. So we're not trying to find more of your everyday skier and snowboarder. We're trying to find more of these kind of core users that we know our products and brand would have a resonance with.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:30): Well, let's back up a little bit. For folks who don't know, can you share a little bit about TREW Who you are and what you guys do?", "token_estimate": 408, "speakers": ["Chris Pew", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:50", "end_timestamp": "02:30", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "a45636c0-e853-4855-86ba-56f6280be5cc", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Chris Pew (02:37): Yeah. We started in, around 2009 was the first year we were selling products, and we started in Hood River, Oregon. And our original product Promise was to create this new type of outerwear for backcountry free ride skiing and snowboarding. So if you think back to 2008, 2009 frame touring bindings were just coming out your marker, duke backcountry at skis were getting super fat and really light at Boots were a thing with walk mode. People were making their own splitboards, some brands were producing, but so there's this hard goods industry for this kind of new type of backcountry skiing, but there wasn't an outerwear kit for that rider. It was like either you're wearing a mountaineering kit or a more traditional Alpine touring kit, which is tight, no pockets lightweight or you had your resort wear that you were wearing outside, and that's what we were doing. (03:37): We were wearing outside of the bounds, we were wearing bonfire pants and an old Marmite Shell or Burton pants and an old arteric shell. We were kind of putting together because we wanted the heft and durability and fit of resort wear, but we wanted something like super waterproof and breathable and durable. So we just put it together and made a baggy kind of backcountry bib. And that product, the Truth Bib has been our best selling product for the past 16 years. There's a specific customer that when they touch and feel our product or find our website, they're going to get it right away. And there's some people that don't. That's totally great. And so our kind of objective on the marketing side is to wave our flag out there and let those customers who are passionate about backcountry skiing in the same way that we are a resort ski in the same way that we are, let 'em know that we're here, this is what we're doing, and these are our values. So that is to your initial question, that is how we want to stand out. We want to stand out to customers in a way that our specific customers can find us, not just like, here we are to everybody because our products aren't for everybody. And some people would buy our products and they'd be like, oh, this is too heavy duty than what I wanted, or I wanted an insulated pan, or something like that, or is this pretty targeted towards these specific users?", "token_estimate": 577, "speakers": ["Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "02:37", "end_timestamp": "02:37", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "a4090ab5-540c-40bc-9cb3-28917578e9d8", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:07): I appreciate that background, that context. That's helpful. When did the idea for the film series, is that what you call it, a film series? Is that\n\nChris Pew (05:14): Yeah, we call it a film contest. We've called it a film project, a community sourced film project. And I mean, that's why it's so fun to talk to you Cole right now because we've done it for two seasons. We're gearing up for our third, and it's really evolved every year. But basically we announced a contest that we're going to be picking 10 teams. There's a large cash prize and gear and other perks from the other sponsors of the contest. And we picked 10 teams and then the teams get eight or nine weeks to film, I think it was maybe eight the first year, nine the second year, nine weeks to film and for six weeks to edit. And the final product can be no longer than five minutes. So it's a five minute edit. The only prompt we gave him was that what I just told you, what's TREW to you? (06:06): So pretty open-ended. We wanted to just see these different creatives and athletes and our industry just put themselves out there and create these unique little vignettes. And then we wanted to put 'em all together. So we stitched them all together, and one 50 is a 55 minute kind of feature presentation, and we do that in the summer to take it out the following fall as like a proper ski film tour. And we take it to 10 different stops, and each stop is the hometown of one of the participating teams. So we basically go to their town or mountain or city and throw them a premier, and then we do a digital release at the same time. And the voting takes place online, but a lot of the voting takes place in person. We put QR codes up so we can see these huge bumps in voting during these in-person events. And then we just tally up the votes and decide the winner and send the winner a check. And that's basically how we've run it for the past two years. Did you get a chance to watch the films, I guess, since we last talked? Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (07:15): Yeah, I did watch some of", "token_estimate": 519, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "05:07", "end_timestamp": "07:15", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "9035aa50-5f07-4224-ab1b-500856ba7b5d", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Chris Pew (07:16): Them. Cool. Yeah, so I'd be curious to talk to you about what it was like viewing, because I think the most interesting part about this project is going out and viewing it, viewing the project with a new audience in these different locations and hearing the different feedback or watching different audiences clap and yell at different times into different parts of the film. And I really dug that. It's like, wow, this is cool. We're taking this thing that can mean different things to different audiences.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:49): I'm going to generalize here. A lot of brands will go out and produce a brand anthem piece, so they'll make a film or they'll tell a story to help contribute to that brand awareness. What you guys did though is you didn't make something and then tell everyone who you were. You passed the mic to other folks and said, Hey, who do you think we are as a collective, this community of folks within the TREW brand? And that's something that I think about a lot, which is it seems like from a brand's position to be able to make something and share it with an audience that takes, I find that to be sometimes presumptuous, to assume that the consumer wants to hear from the brand. Now, there's brands that do this really, really well because they've built a community and they've built trust. But brands who maybe don't have buckets of community building or buckets of trust, that seems like a big hurdle to get over. And I feel like it's one of the missing pieces that brands often don't think about when they're contemplating diving into this world of community building around storytelling. Was that a conscious thought on your guys' behalf to pass the mic or did that just kind of happen by accident and it's worked out really well?", "token_estimate": 446, "speakers": ["Chris Pew", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:16", "end_timestamp": "07:49", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "88fc5847-9fbb-4cba-8d0e-72a36628d4e5", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Chris Pew (09:00): No, that was totally the original idea, and I think it worked really well in this case because part of the message, the brand value that we were trying to promote is creativity and self-expression. So because that's what we're trying to promote, the vehicle that we're distributing that message is actually people being creative. So it's kind of the actual design and structure of this contest promotes that idea in itself. And so that's why I think that works particularly well for this idea or for our brand, rather our brand. We've always tried to, internally, we talk about it, we're a brand for the people. We want to always have this connection to the community and interesting community members. And there's a lot of different facets. We can talk about that concept. And part of it is really about your original question is standing out, right? It's like we're not going to support X Games riders, not because we don't like them or respect them, it's not our value that we're adding to the industry. (10:13): Those individuals are pretty well taken care of by big companies, and big companies should take care of them because responsible for progressing the sport and are responsibility that we think is, we find a lot of value in promoting these unique individuals and lifting up different parts of our community that other parts of the community didn't know exist, and letting everyone check each other out and feel like we're all growing this interesting perspective and outlook together. So yeah, I completely agree with you of your assessment on brand films too. I think the other thing I would say on that is at their best brand films can be incredible, well-produced, well thought out, expanding people's perspectives. They can have messages that uplift parts of communities too. But I would say at their worst brand films are just these kind of self-promoting exercises that every, and we've made some things like this in the past too, that almost if you're an entrepreneur or you're working in a brand, you almost can't resist the urge to tell people what you're doing. (11:29): They're like, I need to tell you why this is so important. I need to tell people why what we're doing is so important. But as a customer or just an audience member, that's not really the most interesting thing ever is to have a brand just tell you why they're so great. Or what even worse than that is having a brand tell you what their values are. So you're like, fine, I get it. Those are your values, but it's more impactful if they can show you if they can be living those values and acting with those values in mind. So I think that's where that comes from. That's why I love this idea, honestly, it has all those things going for it, but then I think where it's going next and what we're really seeing, the energy that the TruView project is creating is it's more of a proper film festival or film contest for the next year. (12:29): Instead of being super connected to our brand, it's like now it seems like it just has this power and momentum to seek out creatives in our industry and give them a platform and give them even more important than a platform is momentum and a prompt. And so I really love that. It's like, okay, now we're transcending our original goal of, hey, help us get our brand out there. And now it's like, oh wow, now we're just helping the community progress and gain new skills and create new interesting content and make the film of their dreams. So yeah, that's kind of its next life. I think that we're hoping season three will start really bringing to the front.", "token_estimate": 903, "speakers": ["Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "09:00", "end_timestamp": "09:00", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "c873fd88-61af-4dd8-86b5-9d21c9c4d9ca", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:22): That's cool. I mean, you guys are play, you're playing in culture, you're starting to define a culture, and it seems like people are noticing and it's starting to have a life of its own. How many contestants submitted for season two, and how many do you think will attempt to get into season three? Just so maybe folks have an idea of how many filmmakers want to work with Drew on this project?\n\nChris Pew (13:50): Yeah, yeah. I think the first season we had less than a hundred applications and we've really pushed it. We were just calling our friends be like, Hey, do you know anyone that wants to join this thing? And that's how we put the first season together and maybe we had 60 or 70 applications, and the next year we had around 160, 170 applications and we picked 10 teams out of those applications. And the process of picking the teams is, as you can see from the participants of last season, is a geographical diversity. We wanted teams from these different communities. The most interesting part about skiing and snowboarding is skiing in Michigan is way different than skiing in Oregon. So that's your first thing that you want to put together. And then next we wanted to look at diversity of discipline. Are we going to want snowboarders and skiers? First season, we made a telemark team. It was radical from Colorado, didn't have Telemark team second season change, is there backcountry skiers? Are there people that are just in resort? Like this past season, we had this guy Ryan from Lake Tahoe who made an incredible film. He's a lifelong snowboarder, and he just wanted to see if he can teach himself how to ski in 30 days. And he filmed his journey and did an incredible job. So you show the learning\n\nCole Heilborn (15:15): Progression. I haven't seen that one. I'll have to go back and check it", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "13:22", "end_timestamp": "15:15", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "e5d4439d-c3ac-4ef6-be10-00dd102ef91d", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Chris Pew (15:18): Out. That one's called Old Dog. Ryan's a really talented commercial filmmaker in his own. So we're also looking for, we're evaluating that, right? Have they made films? Have they demonstrated their ability to build a finished product? But then also we want to make sure we don't only prioritize that, and we have made an emphasis on getting participants in the past couple of years that haven't made a film. That's kind of like the point, if you have an idea and you have something to say, then here's the momentum to bring that to life.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:03): What do you want it to become? What's the vision for the contest?\n\nChris Pew (16:08): That's a great question. I mean, I think that's evolving too. Like I said, initially, the vision for this, the purpose of this contest was purely brand awareness. We wanted to have people help us get this cool perspective out into the world that we felt like our brand had or has. And today, like I said, I think the vision has evolved to being kind of a standalone film contest in the snow industry that rewards creativity and storytelling. And it's interesting. Do you watch ski films and snowboard films and stuff? So yeah, you're like everyone else in the snow industry. And how old are you, Cole?\n\nCole Heilborn (17:01): I'm 30.", "token_estimate": 325, "speakers": ["Chris Pew", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:18", "end_timestamp": "17:01", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}, {"id": "5e7cd19d-fbfb-4d44-8df9-322342a19e66", "episode_id": "8fe50214-e3d5-42f0-a033-e0336f5f08fb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4961", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Chris Pew (17:02): You're 30, okay. Yeah, I'm a little older. I'm 38. But you're right in that same kind of era as me, right? We straddled VHS and DVD, and that's how we got ski films. And then today it's Instagram and YouTube. And so when you think about the ski films that we grew up with, they serve so many important purposes in our industry, and one of them was progression. Every fall the ski films would come out and we'd just be glued to 'em. We'd buy the VHS or we'd go to the premiers. And one of the things I was most interested in seeing is like, okay, what's possible? What's everyone doing? What's going on? There weren't twin tips and there's twin tips, and then there's Big Mountain progression was crazy when we were teenagers, just like what was Seth Morrison sha of Con and Ken Kreitler going to do next? (17:56): And that's the only place you could see it. So we dissected these ski films, but today it happens in real time all the time on YouTube and Instagram. And it's incredible to be able to watch that if someone does another flip or whatever, or another crazy handrail that no one else had done before, we see it right away. Some of the film companies are guarding their best shots for the fall releases, but even that, it's becoming less common. Some of the most interesting progression is just happening in real time on YouTube. And so I would say that isn't the primary objective of our ski foam being taken care of in the creator sphere of all these incredible athletes. And so I look at the other things that are the ski films added when we were growing up, and I think there's community and there's storytelling. (18:54): And I would say the community part of ski films has degraded. I wouldn't say it's been lost, but I mean, the best example of this is the old Warren Miller films, and he was an ambassador to skiing. He's like the elder statesman of ski media. And the films today are not a TREW embodiment of, I don't think I'm being controversial when I'm saying that then they're really more of a corporate exercise. But the films made before the year 2000, especially when Warren was still involved, or even earlier in the early nineties and late eighties where Warren narrated, or even before that when he went on tour and narrated in person, which I wasn't alive for, but his films were an overview of skiing and snowboarding, the thrilling parts of it, like the best athletes doing extreme skiing or freestyle, which is just starting to happen. (19:52): Were put right alongside the Bunny Hill scenes and the comedy of learning to ski, the comedy of a rope toe in the Midwest and the comedy of early snowboarding before it was just a nascent part of our industry, the comedy of fashion, all these different parts of the ski industry, you'd see it in one film. And I found that to be exhilarating as a kid, if you're not traveling around to all these different places, you just get to see it all in one spot. And today, just a lot of our different parts of our media world, there's different silos of content and silos of skiing and snowboarding. If all you click on is urban skiing, snowboarding, then that's all you're going to see and you're going to think that's the whole world, which yields really impressive results for that specific community. Now these different silos are really, really growing, but that's one of the outcomes I would say that wasn't anywhere near to anything we were trying to do. (21:05): But that's an observation that I had when we were on tour. I was like, wow, we're mashing up all these different communities and we're just putting them on the screen. And in a few cases, in some of the tours, tour stops, we had members from different teams there at the same time hyping each other up. The Dominican snowboarders got to meet one of the filmers from the SCH swack pack from Vermont at the New York City premiere last year, and they were just gassing each other up and they're excited about what they were doing. And I'm just sitting there thinking, man, these are, they would've not only would've been unusual to just even meet in person, they're just in different parts of the country skiing in different parts of the country, but even their content, you're not going to just stumble across the Dominican snowboarder team. (21:57): If you're like the sch swack pack, you're ski the East, you're Vermont, you're looking at all types of ski media. But to be able to mash these up and just kind of see what happens and let it become this new product, I think was really interesting. And so this is a really long-winded answer, but that's the vision, is we want this to be the overview of this certain type of writing. And I think the benefit that could come of it is just interesting audience building and better content. These different creators get to meet each other and get motivated by each other and create a product that was bigger than the sum of its parts. And I say that's the goal.", "token_estimate": 1242, "speakers": ["Chris Pew"], "start_timestamp": "17:02", "end_timestamp": "17:02", "podcast_index": 195, "episode_name": "TREW Gear - Creating a Branded Film Festival", "slug": "trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/trew-gear---creating-a-branded-film-festival", "guest_name": "Chris Pew", "guest_title": "Ceo and Co-Founder of TREW Gear", "categories": ["creative", "brand"], "short_description": "Chris shares how TREW is fostering deeper connections with its core audience through storytelling and innovative community engagement strategies. The conversation spans the evolution of ski film culture, the role of social media in building authentic communities, and how small brands can leverage creativity to make a big impact.", "podcast_length": "39:29"}]
